window.harnessChunks["rows-de646521aa368772.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:59:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FAHTHIZ/oasis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OASIS</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FAHTHIZ/oasis/tree/b03a4207c6969a71d570cedf4db2651245b4a822\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b03a4207c6969a71d570cedf4db2651245b4a822\">b03a420 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Local autonomous coding task execution with tool observations and completion feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Codebase uncertainty, tool errors, unexpected test failures, model tool-output malformation and incomplete edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model selects and revises file/search/shell/Git/tool operations from task observations; may request child agents. - Decision owner: Chosen parent or task-specific model agent, not the provider endpoint or deterministic safety layer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runAgentTurnRaw step loop, executeTool dispatcher, permissions, tool-call repair, session history, file checkpoints and verification state. - Closure path: Model tool-call→runtime authorization/execution→tool result→subsequent model turn, with final response checked against actual diff/test evidence. - Boundary reachability: Normal shipped OASIS CLI/web/SDK session and task-subagent APIs, not an evaluation-only mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Host safety gates constrain and verify, but cannot choose the task-sensitive next action without the model. - Evidence: agent.mjs#L4633, agent.mjs#L5019, tools.mjs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for pinned first-party loop wiring, not independent quality/effectiveness. - Caveats: No paid/local model run was performed; model availability and permissions vary by deployment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Multiple model workers launched through task/background dispatch, with separate context and optional isolated worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two workers attempting to claim/change the same shared task, duplicate work after an expired claim, and downstream work starting before a prerequisite is complete. - Attenuating coordination relation: Shared task leases/dependency checks plus a model-accessible tasklist, taskclaim, taskrelease, taskupdate, teamsendmessage and conditional dispatcher. Parent/model and workers can select alternative ready work or explicitly release/reassign after observing a conflict. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Claim rejection names current claimant/expiry, successful release/expiry opens a task for reassignment, and prerequisite status changes readiness for subsequent dispatch; agent receives these tool results before its next choice. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Exclusive ownership and dependency constraints are designed to attenuate concrete conflicting work, not just order generic messages; conflict feedback alters which worker can perform which task next. - Function: Inter-worker mutual-exclusion, dependency and conflict-adjustment regulation at task-board recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate edits/claims, incompatible simultaneous work and premature dependent launches. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and adjust shared-task ownership/dependencies and reassign after claim refusal or worker failure. - Decision owner: Model agents exercising first-party board and team tools, including parent with explicit force-release authority; runtime owns only atomic lease enforcement and safe dispatch limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session-local board with lease TTL, dependency DAG, agent registry/status/events, per-worker worktrees and dispatch waves. - Closure path: Collision/expired claim or failed worker→board tool status and event to agent→new claim, release, reassignment or changed dependency→next eligible worker execution. - Boundary reachability: Shipped default task and dispatchreadytasks tools are exposed to live parent/worker sessions, not developer-only examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The code enforces accepted decisions, while autonomous agents choose task selection, claim/release and response to returned contention information; simply auto-serializing alone is not credited. - Evidence: shared-tasks.mjs#L232, agent.mjs#L3552, agent-tasks.mjs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the supported multi-agent mode; no reproduced competing-work test in this review. - Caveats: A session that never launches peers has no active S2 relation; leases are session-local and reset on process restoration. A failed automatic dispatch pauses; agent discretion to adjust is available but not guaranteed to be effective.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Parent model can inspect the full shared task board, claims, dependencies and all worker reports/status/events within its session, including worktree changes awaiting integration. - Current-control decision scope: Create/prioritize task commitments, change dependencies/owners, choose dispatch parallelism, pause or resume waves, intervene in failures and judge whether to integrate worker branches. - Function: Current whole-session commitments and resource allocation across delegated code work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Blocked dependencies, worker failure, depleted agent slots, pending unintegrated changes and unfinished work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The main model changes current task definitions/owners, manages waves and adjusts continuation from observed worker evidence. - Decision owner: Main OASIS model agent, subject to operator approval for unsafe tool/merge operations; runtime only enforces preselected guardrails. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tasklist/update, agentstatus/events, dispatch limit and pause logic, Git worktrees and explicit review/integration workflow. - Closure path: Workers finish/fail→agent event/board read→main model updates commitments or dispatches/reviews next wave→new worker state or a blocked escalation. - Boundary reachability: First-party task, agent and dispatch tools are available in an ordinary multi-agent OASIS session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The dispatcher computes feasible slots but does not decide task priorities, exception response or integration; those are model/current-control choices. - Evidence: agent.mjs#L549, agent.mjs#L3431, agent.mjs#L3552. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium; supported current-control topology, not measured resilience of a real swarm. - Caveats: No sustained service across process restart; external manual worktree integration is required. Human intervention rights at tool gates do not alone qualify a separate S3 parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Parent or implementation worker&#39;s claim that a code change is correct or free of relevant security regressions. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementing model returns its own final prose, tool transcript, diff and verification state to parent/main session. - Complementary access path: Distinct read-only code-review / security-review model session or bounded runspecialistreview directly reads actual repository files and Git evidence with independent tool context, then returns finding details. - Independence boundary: Specialist has a separate session, no inherited working conversation as authority, read-only tool allowlist and explicit prohibition on edits, shell/network and MCP; distinct built-in read-only reviewers can be invoked through task. This is procedural separation, not institutional isolation or a mandatory third-party audit. - Who acts on findings: Parent/main agent receives reviewer result in the normal tool response, inspects reported paths and applies or delegates corrections, followed by available tests/verification. - Function: Independent challenge of work claims from actual source/diff evidence, with returned corrective action potential. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible but incorrect implementation summaries, untested edge cases and security regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separate specialist model judges inspected code and reports bounded actionable findings; main session owns remediation decisions. - Decision owner: Read-only review model owns independent audit judgment, distinct from production agent; parent owns current-control correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runSpecialistReview, specialist roles, bounded review budgets, reviewer read-only tool filter and ordinary verification gate. - Closure path: Main invokes review→separate model reads actual files/diff→returns concrete findings→main re-evaluates and revises code or task→checks again. - Boundary reachability: Built-in specialist-review tool and code-review/security-review subagents available without custom extensions, in normal coding sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The specialist—not the implementer or its deterministic verifier—chooses what to challenge and whether evidence supports a defect. - Evidence: agent.mjs#L2982, agent.mjs#L4211, subagents.mjs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium for reachable complementary path; not a measured audit-success rate. - Caveats: Review is optional, and some tool/approval modes may narrow its usefulness. Deterministic auto-test and LSP checks are excluded from S3 credit by themselves.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Prospective external/environmental sensing and governing self-adaptation of the OASIS session capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Evolving APIs, model catalogs and ecosystem plugins. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No evidenced first-party external future assessment→adaptation option→committed capability update loop under an autonomous S4 actor. - Decision owner: Operator/developer selects model, extension updates and imported skills; task-agent searches are for the current user deliverable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model discovery/router, web search, update command, plugin catalog, imported skill review, session memory and workflow scripts. - Closure path: Configuration/skills change by operator action or a current task&#39;s explicit command; there is no distinct prospective environmental function returning to whole-session S3 capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Availability of web tools, plugin import and a roadmap does not establish self-directed future adaptation of the harness. - Evidence: architecture, update.mjs, extensions.mjs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for bounded standard modes examined. - Caveats: A custom workflow may research future changes, but that composition is not a shipped autonomous S4 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Ultimate identity/policy conflict resolution for the OASIS agent system itself. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing goals and exceptional decisions over core system identity, not ordinary tool safety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party identity-level legitimacy mandate and binding returned policy decision. - Decision owner: User/developer authors permissions, model/tool settings, static policy text and task instructions; model cannot redefine ultimate authority through the workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission policy, plan/approval gates, trust boundaries, security patterns and protected paths. - Closure path: Approval or rejection returns to individual tool/task execution; no identity dispute reaches a legitimized ultimate authority and returns to govern subsequent system purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even stringent deterministic safety checks regulate risky acts, not the identity or ultimate policy of a viable system. - Evidence: permissions.mjs, agent.mjs, architecture. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Human project governance outside the runtime is not asserted absent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4224e16c244d760835866078fe52634c8283401f/assessments/oa/oasis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4224e16c244d760835866078fe52634c8283401f\">4224e16 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T20:24:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Jero110/pi-house\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pi-house</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Jero110/pi-house/tree/5c456584931025f421eb45c7232683737a628371\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5c456584931025f421eb45c7232683737a628371\">5c45658 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out plan-scoped code tasks and evaluate local acceptance results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task-specific code state, unforeseen implementation obstacles, command results and integration errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The dispatched model worker selects edits, tools, tests and repairs within its stated task contract. - Decision owner: Individual model-bearing agent process working under the first-party executor/agent-link protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pinned plan, task brief, harness-native coding interface, isolated worktree, result routing and test commands. - Closure path: Task brief → worker inspects source → edits and tests → reports result/commit → executor verification and possible repair → accepted code. - Boundary reachability: The shipped planner/executor skills and agent-link spawn route instantiate this ordinary mode through installed pih, not through an unrelated downstream user application. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Workers adapt code/tool choices and failures; the hub and test executable alone do not choose fixes. Model cognition belongs to installed external runtimes but the task execution operating loop is explicitly first-party assembled. - Evidence: executor skill, Steps 1–3, architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the shipped protocol; no provider-backed run executed for this review. - Caveats: External agent/model availability and the user&#39;s task quality remain outside ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent model-bearing coding workers, each assigned a task, branch/worktree, and owned files. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two otherwise valid workers editing shared files, violating prerequisite order, clashing during integration or exhausting a shared quota pool. - Attenuating coordination relation: The executor checks dependency and file independence before fanout, assigns disjoint ownership, defers shared wiring, revises dispatch after results and resolves isolated integration conflicts; the hub relays workers&#39; status and feedback. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A conflicting or unverified result is not declared done; the executor revises task integration or asks the worker for a corrected result, and only independent ready tasks continue concurrently. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The executor skill names inter-worker file conflicts and dependent work as disturbances, requiring model-owned independence judgment and changed commitments, above deterministic message relay and FIFO routing. - Function: Attenuate collisions among S1 workers contributing to one plan. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping edits, premature dependent work, model-account budget contention, and inconsistent integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide which tasks can safely run simultaneously, partition files and change worker/merge commitments when feedback exposes interference. - Decision owner: Model-bearing executor; individual workers respond to revised briefs and correction requests. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dependency graph, per-task worktrees, assigned owned files, global quota-backed worker cap, agent-link result delivery and merge verification. - Closure path: Plan/worktree overlap identified → executor allocates or delays workers → detects conflict/result → adjusts task/merge order or issues correction → subsequent workers proceed under new ownership. - Boundary reachability: skills/build/executor/SKILL.md is part of the installed standard planner-to-executor flow, and hub spawn/results are first-party tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic worktree isolation cannot decide semantic independence; the executor is instructed to inspect dependencies and ownership and act on real feedback. - Evidence: executor skill, Step 1 and Step 3, hub.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the documented deployed path, not measured conflict success. - Caveats: No S2 attribution to the FIFO hub alone or the external harnesses&#39; generic team features.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: The executor reads the complete validated plan, progress ledger, dependency state, quota observations and returned worker results. - Current-control decision scope: Allocate current tasks to workers, choose authorized model routes, adjust concurrency/priority on failures, integrate branches and determine verified completion versus gaps. - Function: Manage present project-plan commitments and recovery across S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Blocked prerequisites, failed results, exhausted accounts, unverified work and integration deviations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Dispatch independent ready tasks; pause blocked work; choose one bounded retry/correction; integrate accepted changes and decide the plan&#39;s completion report. - Decision owner: Model-bearing executor under the user-approved plan; user holds plan/goal authority outside this S3 autonomous current-control path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan ledger, usage observations, worker reports/transcripts, integration branch, validation commands, and limits enforced by router/hub. - Closure path: Current plan and progress → executor observes availability/results → assigns/pauses/repairs commitments → integrates verified code → reports whole-plan status. - Boundary reachability: The default planner handoff opens the executor pane and runs the installed executor protocol; no separate management application needs to implement the decision loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The executor makes substantive current resource/commitment choices; the router, worker cap and test exit codes only constrain/enforce those decisions. - Evidence: executor skill, Steps 0–4, workflow.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for procedural current-control rights, no measured end-to-end success in this review. - Caveats: Parent-approved plan decisions and quotas are not silently reassigned to the executor as S5 governance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: A worker&#39;s reported task completion, validation evidence and claimed changes/commit. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker sends STATUS, summary, validation output, commit and concerns to its parent executor. - Complementary access path: The separate executor independently runs the task&#39;s validation in the worker checkout, checks missing evidence once, reads transcript/state if needed, and returns a bounded correction or records a gap. - Independence boundary: Executor is a separate model-bearing agent from the S1 task owner and checks actual checkout state. It is also S3 current-control owner, not an external third-party auditor; its independent inspection is task-level, not certification of the whole system. - Who acts on findings: The executor requests a correction from the same worker (one retry), otherwise records an unresolved gap, and refuses to integrate an unverified task. - Function: Challenge worker success reports using independent current evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect PASS summaries, absent validation, untested code and integration claims that disagree with actual repository state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Accept, recheck, refuse or return worker work for correction based on direct checkout validation. - Decision owner: The separate model-bearing executor inspecting the S1 worker&#39;s artifacts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run validation yourself, worktree access, worker brief&#39;s acceptance criteria, one-retry policy, ledger and reporting gaps. - Closure path: Worker claims PASS → executor runs validation against actual worktree → failure or missing evidence returns to worker → corrected artifact is retested or marked a gap before integration. - Boundary reachability: Independent executor check is mandatory in the installed executor skill&#39;s Step 2; the worker and parent run as distinct agent-link sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The command exit code is mechanical, but deciding which claims lack evidence, asking for additional evidence and directing corrections are assigned to the executor; no claim of third-party independence is made. - Evidence: executor skill, Step 2 and Step 3, architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; independent internal check is mandated, external audit independence is not. - Caveats: Test selection derives from the plan; this is complementary task-work audit, not a proved independent S3 organization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - External distinction: Prior completed workflow runs, token/skill-use observations, recurring failure patterns and retained run digests are inputs distinct from current task execution. - Future / prospective distinction: Bounded review generates proposed changes to skills or scoped memory intended to improve later runs, not to repair the current checkout immediately. - Adaptation option generated: Separate scan/review worker suggests concrete before/after changes with supporting/opposing digest evidence, uncertainty and validation. - Path back into current capability / S3: Human reviews an immutable proposal version, approves it explicitly, then the system applies an allowlisted version-checked skill/memory change with validation and receipt; future agent invocations can retrieve/use the approved content. - Function: Parent-authorized development of future harness skills and reusable operational memory from retrospective environment evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeated skill misuse, avoidable failed runs and loss of operational experience across future tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authorize or reject the proposed future change; no automatic model self-modification. - Decision owner: Legitimate local human operator, acting as parent of this local harness instance; scan/review agents propose but do not own approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Manual /workflow self-learning, separate digest/review workers, scoped target allowlist, version-pinned approval, compare-and-swap apply, validation and receipt. - Closure path: Captured run evidence → agent digest/review proposal → human version-pinned approve → code applies/validates → changed reusable skill or memory becomes available to subsequent S1/S3 tasks. - Boundary reachability: /workflow self-learning, /workflow proposals, /workflow approve and the implementation of version-pinned applyProposal are public built-in commands; this is an operational local parent mode, not a hypothetical external governance overlay. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents make prospective recommendations, but the decisive durable capability choice is explicitly reserved for the human; parent mode is positive without inventing autonomous S4 approval. - Evidence: self-learning.md, learning-review.ts, proposals.ts, workflow.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded parent-mode loop; no claim of automatic long-term adaptation quality. - Caveats: Learning is manually invoked and bounded to allowlisted targets; general environmental forecasting and autonomous policy changes are not evidenced.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party owner is evidenced for resolving contested purpose, ultimate policy or identity and returning a legitimate binding organizational decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Account/model authorization and plan ownership are boundaries fixed externally, not internal constitutional disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Redefine the system&#39;s binding purpose or policy by an accountable agent or local higher-recursion authority; not supplied. - Decision owner: Human user establishes plan decisions, model/account authorization and local approval of bounded skills; no S5 purpose-adjudication actor within the assessed harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planner user approval, routing allowlists, workflow proposal approvals and limits on remote worker actions. - Closure path: Current plan governance and bounded S4 proposal authorization change operations/capabilities but do not provide an identity/policy question → constitutional authority → binding return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A human approving a skill patch is not itself a legitimate organizational S5 policy court; the executor cannot override the goal or create new model spending authority. - Evidence: executor skill, self-learning.md, architecture.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the reviewed operating modes. - Caveats: A human/project maintainer may exercise external governance; this does not establish a first-party S5 return at the local operational recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/49cd03d3257ac0af76d21868ccb44c006f07086c/assessments/pi/pi-house.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 49cd03d3257ac0af76d21868ccb44c006f07086c\">49cd03d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:47:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yizhi0107-bot/opencode-jarvis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCode Jarvis</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yizhi0107-bot/opencode-jarvis/tree/fb8e47dc6525ec4ff4b4ef333ae7a8d8420c49d7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fb8e47dc6525ec4ff4b4ef333ae7a8d8420c49d7\">fb8e47d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute code, research, debugging and documentation subtasks through model-bearing specialist workers and tool result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown files, failing tests, coding defects, unclear task scope and errors returned by tools or delegated roles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select file modifications, investigations, debug hypotheses, test commands and worker responses within an assigned scoped ticket. - Decision owner: Each specialist LLM role, notably executor/debugger for code writes and architect/scout/scribe for analysis; OpenCode transports actual tool calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role-specific tools and permissions, bounded steps, task instructions, acceptance criteria, ticketed subagent launch and test reporting. - Closure path: Parent issues ticket/task → specialist runs model-selected work and observes file/test results → child reports result → parent consumes output and may assign correction. - Boundary reachability: Plugin setup installs the bundled roles via installResources and agent.reload; Jarvis commands and first-party agent prompt delegate to them through the OpenCode host. The claim requires running the declared host, not external maintainer dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Worker model chooses substantive edits/investigations. The ticket gate restricts scope/parallelism but does not implement the code. - Evidence: executor, debugger, installer, tickets. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for shipped decision path, medium for actual provider outcome. - Caveats: Models and OpenCode host runtime were not live exercised here; this is a source-level review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Agent-governed adjustment of contemporaneous specialist work to avoid collisions over shared file scopes and tier capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two executor/debugger workers target overlapping files, or local/strong concurrency cap is exhausted while the parent seeks another dispatch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: After refusal, choose to wait, narrow scope, group independent work, change worker or dispatch order, then issue a revised ticket; repeated collision is no longer blindly launched. - Decision owner: Strong-model Jarvis parent chooses changed task boundaries/timing; plugin ticket guard detects and enforces collisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ticket map with scopeConflict, writesFiles classifier, per-tier caps, dispatch refusal messages, permission hook denying subagents with no issued ticket, completion/TTL cleanup. - Closure path: Active writer ticket creates occupied scope → new overlapping dispatch rejected with actual ticket ID/reason → Jarvis receives refusal and is instructed to revise scope/wait → revised dispatch, or changed sequence, allows nonconflicting work → completion frees scope. - Boundary reachability: src/index.ts registers the governance plugin hooks, and the installed Jarvis role explicitly requires ticketed dispatch and instructs the model to respond to rejection rather than simply repeat it; this is a first-party standard-distribution path. - Distinct S1 units: Named executor and debugger (and other local/strong workers) dispatched as separate model-bearing subagents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping writer file scopes and excessive simultaneously active workers. - Attenuating coordination relation: Substantive model selection of a revised scope or time coupled to a first-party ticket collision guard. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Returned rejected ticket or completed-ticket audit changes the next dispatch, so subsequent workers run with non-overlapping scopes or deferred timing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It identifies competing file ownership of two actual worker types and supplies explicit conflict refusal plus returned agent-directed correction; ordinary subagent routing alone would not be credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Collision detection is deterministic, but the living Jarvis model owns the correction and subsequent assignment, making this A in the documented operating mode. - Evidence: tickets collision and issue, tickets hooks, Jarvis discipline, workers. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high for installed per-process tickets. - Caveats: Scope matching is string/path-prefix-based and can miss semantically shared state; it does not prove cross-process or cross-organization conflict isolation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Jarvis allocates and revises current work across the specialized agent portfolio in one managed task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task difficulty variation, local-model failure, worker resource saturation, poor implementation quality and mismatched work commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decompose task, choose route/tier and worker, set goal/scope/acceptance, decide parallel vs sequential dispatch and escalate executor failures to debugger or reviewer. - Decision owner: Primary strong-model Jarvis in the installed role; human sets initial task and broad resource limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Explicit primary agent with no edit/shell permission, eight worker roster, local/strong tier caps, dispatch ticket lifecycle, route-confidence fallback and scoped result summaries. - Closure path: Current user goal and worker feedback/refusals → Jarvis assesses whole local task and active commitments → changes worker, scope, order or escalation → ticketed child action → returned results for further current decisions. - Boundary reachability: Plugin installs a primary Jarvis agent and dispatch tool, and documented --agent jarvis or TUI selection reaches the agent instructions; /deep assigns a concrete sequence. No independent external PM is required. - Whole-system current view: The supervising Jarvis conversation owns its task decomposition, issued ticket IDs, child returns, failure/rejection feedback and, when requested, aggregated /jarvis-stats telemetry. This is a local one-task composition view, not a cross-session global operations center. - Current-control decision scope: Allocation of current subtask work by model tier, worker, acceptance criteria, file scope, concurrency/ordering and escalation after failure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Jarvis decides substantive current priorities and reassignments; deterministic ticket limits enforce rather than decide the portfolio plan. - Evidence: Jarvis primary role, deep workflow, tickets. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium at declared task recursion; no global whole-project oversight claim. - Caveats: Ticket active map remains internal to one plugin process; operational view is parent conversation and its task, not all users or projects.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate read-only reviewer role independently checks implemented code and tests, returning repair findings to the task controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An executor may claim a successful change despite defects, regression risks, boundary gaps or insufficient tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect real files/test outcomes distinct from executor narrative, judge severity and return issues for correcting the actual implementation. - Decision owner: Jarvis/reviewer model, separate from writer executor and constrained to read/grep/glob plus user-confirmed shell tests; Jarvis orders returned corrections. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer role permission boundary denying edits, dedicated reviewerModel option, /deep mandated review and severity-triggered executor fix + one re-review, /council independent judge inputs. - Closure path: Executor reports implemented diff → separate reviewer reads working source and may run checks → reviewer returns high/medium findings with file:line/severity → Jarvis sends executor correction → reviewer rechecks, then final report. - Boundary reachability: Bundled reviewer and /deep templates are installed in global/project scope by the first-party installer; the exact installed command explicitly mandates reviewer and returned fix/review. No external CI auditor is borrowed. - Claim being audited: Correctness, regressions, boundaries, maintainability and test evidence for this task&#39;s implementation. - Ordinary reporting path: Executor&#39;s changed-file and verification report to Jarvis after its assigned work. - Complementary access path: Reviewer uses independent tool calls to read/search changed files and, with permission, run tests/static checks. - Independence boundary: Separate model role and tool restrictions (read-only; cannot edit); reviewer does not merely repeat executor&#39;s output, but its subprocess still shares the workspace and is not an organizationally independent external auditor. - Who acts on findings: Jarvis directs executor to repair high/medium issues and orders one subsequent reviewer pass in /deep mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewing model makes its own issue/severity judgment from code; controller/host pass its findings to an explicit correction path. - Evidence: reviewer role, deep workflow, council workflow. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium for documented correction ownership; direct provider-level independence and efficacy not runtime tested. - Caveats: A bundled command instructing review is not itself evidence of actual effective audit in every provider run. CLI must explicitly select --agent jarvis for the /deep pathway, as README warns.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No installed prospective external environment sensing and future system capability adaptation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Cost/quality routing, output limits and failed tools are handled for the present request. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Form and select a strategic future adaptation option and return it to current capability governance; not supplied. - Decision owner: Jarvis selects current task route; operator/maintainer changes models/options and versions outside a first-party prospective policy cycle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Triage confidence fallback, model availability warning, metrics audit and /jarvis-stats historical aggregation, setup/reload command. - Closure path: Metrics are visible to a user and routing decisions affect present task; no automatic or mandatory future adaptation selection and capability-return channel is closed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Cheap-vs-strong model choice is an immediate current allocation (S3), not automatically outside-and-then strategic intelligence. - Evidence: Jarvis prompt, commands/stats, options. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Human may analyze historical routing metrics and change configuration manually; this is not a separately closed S4 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No separate ultimate identity or policy adjudication with authoritative returned governance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unauthorized dispatch and unsafe commands are bounded by fixed plugin ticket and role permission rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve a continuing identity/ultimate-policy conflict and issue binding policy for later organizational operations; absent. - Decision owner: Maintainer-designed Jarvis purpose and role templates, human-selected installation scope/models; tickets enforce operational rules rather than a changing S5 mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: User configuration, agentScope global/project/off, role permissions, no-ticket dispatch refusal, selected local/strong models. - Closure path: User selects options or model and reloads; call gates execute configured rules. No S5 identity question → legitimate ultimate authority judgment → binding policy-return feedback loop is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fixed local-first identity and per-call permission policies do not create self-governing S5; config changes alone do not establish P. - Evidence: options, installer, tickets. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: OpenCode&#39;s broader host or a user&#39;s project governance may supply policy elsewhere but is not this plugin&#39;s credited S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/07514974531a8af0ddc884c15695b06084b7ab0b/assessments/op/opencode-jarvis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 07514974531a8af0ddc884c15695b06084b7ab0b\">0751497 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T20:51:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TaewiKim/autoresearchlab\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AutoResearchLab</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TaewiKim/autoresearchlab/tree/bd70a67aafa172a4e7c5a3f4477d6e438cfeb0d8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bd70a67aafa172a4e7c5a3f4477d6e438cfeb0d8\">bd70a67 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous role lanes produce scoped scientific work: locate and evaluate evidence, design a falsifiable experiment, provision execution, run analysis, and write defensible results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar academic domains, unavailable tools/datasets, conflicting data quality, unanticipated engineering failures, analytical alternatives and result uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Scout agents choose/refine field-specific queries, Analyst chooses estimands and tests, Engineer chooses how to provision the selected toolchain without altering the science, and Runner writes/runs the contract-bound analysis; their outputs determine subsequent experimental work. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed role agents operating through the first-party lane contracts; external Codex CLI executes the model sessions but its mere availability does not establish additional first-party S-functions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust lane dispatch, role-scoped sandboxes, sealed contracts/artifacts, runtime state and deterministic validity checks. - Closure path: Role plan → agent work/result artifact → contract and gate checks → next role uses the output or reports a blocker to the director → subsequent experiment/review changes accordingly. - Boundary reachability: The shipped CLI and agent plan construct these Codex role lanes, the native harness runs them and persists returned results; these are not example-only or CI-only role stubs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents possess bounded scientific and implementation discretion; queue/budget enforcement and filesystem persistence are deterministic supports, not the source of their scientific choices. - Evidence: role rights; reasoning roles in shipped plan; native lane entry. - Basis: Structural shipped agent-role/decision and result-return paths, supplemented by explicit source-owning architecture descriptions. - Confidence: High for first-party configurability and decision ownership, not a measurement of model effectiveness. - Caveats: No live Codex/LLM agent, scholarly API or actual research experiment was run in this assessment; science quality is not a VSM autonomy grade.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: ? - Function: Candidate stabilization of cross-role experiment/review work, but a separately established inter-S1 oscillation-attenuation function remains ambiguous at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing author experiment requests, engineering/analysis handoff dependencies, status messages crowding other channels and duplicate/superseded work may disrupt delivery to other scientific units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The bus carries author→director requests and enforces per-channel capacity, unique identity, delivery receipts and escalation; LabDirector can adopt/decline experiments with reasons. It is not established that the latter is a distinct inter-S1 damping decision rather than S3 prioritization and sequential task handoff. - Decision owner: Agents answer substantive requests, but deterministic bus/scheduler implements capacity, escalation and delivery; S2-specific ownership is unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Request/standing channel registry, per-channel quotas, stateful deduplication, escalations, lane sequencing and contract-bound handoffs. - Closure path: Author request → bus → director response → changed experiment/document plan is plausible, but the reviewed evidence does not separate this from hierarchical current-control return sufficiently for an autonomous S2 claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Actual feedback messages and decisions exist; their S2-specific function and direct reciprocal stabilization of distinct operational units cannot be inferred merely from those mechanisms. - Evidence: author request with owed reason; per-channel capacity and delivery; projection and return; documented channel history. - Basis: Structural coordination candidates; unknown function-specific inter-S1 relation. - Confidence: Medium that coordination machinery exists; low on attribution of a separate S2 function. - Caveats: The specific unresolved witness is two independently operating S1 cells whose mutual interference, rather than a parent backlog/approval dependency, is attenuated through an accountable, closed S2 path.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate present scientific commitments and next work for the entire current research program, responding to failed reviews, stalled runs, evidence gaps and competing follow-up demands. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Expensive repeated analyses, weak lead hypotheses, unresolved gate blockers, scientific stagnation, contradictory handoffs and limited lane budget/time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: LabDirector selects/refines/pivots/concludes a research lead and chooses follow-up scientific tasks, based on Chronicle, current gate results and supervisor notices; it may not override independently recorded reviewer scores. - Decision owner: The model-backed LabDirector agent for scientific priority/correction. Supervisor stall detection, Rust dispatch/termination, budgets and hard rubric gates constrain or request action but do not own that scientific decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cycle-wide summaries and notes, request escalations, lane priority/dispatch, budget and stagnation guards, protected lead memory, append-only feedback. - Closure path: Whole-cycle evidence/active review blocker → director&#39;s scientific correction/pivot/priority decision → role task/lead updates → scheduler launches affected subsequent lanes → newer results are reviewed again. - Boundary reachability: The default agent plan includes a LabDirector across stages, and the native cycle uses its lead decisions and reviewer feedback to create follow-ups; not a maintainer-only or external governance workflow. - Whole-system current view: Chronicle digest, evidence/decision notes, active blockers, gate review and lane/cycle states across the same research-program boundary. - Current-control decision scope: Next research work, which lead/claim is current, which further measurements or corrections are owed, and whether to pivot or conclude under the fixed scientific and resource constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The director has substantive authority to adjudicate scientific commitments; a deterministic forced-stall request cannot replace its selection of a scientifically justified next hypothesis. - Evidence: director role and limits; agent plan duty; active blocker review return; source-bound strategy decisions. - Basis: Explicit role contract plus structural gate→decision→followup path. - Confidence: Medium-high for source-wired current control; no live effectiveness evidence. - Caveats: Forced livelock termination and resource enforcement are deterministic. Owner instructions can constrain individual choices, but ordinary operator steering does not by itself establish a distinct closed S3 parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge the producing research roles&#39; hypothesis, validity, technical/reproducibility and reporting claims before acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Novelty overclaims, unsound analysis, stale evidence, unreplicated results, and a production lane&#39;s optimistic interpretation of its own outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separately dispatched Gatekeeper model agents assess current bound evidence and return approve/challenge/reject, rubric scores and missing validations; their judgments cannot be overwritten by LabDirector. - Decision owner: Independent Gatekeeper agent lanes; deterministic rubric parsing, conjunctive gates and scheduling enforce the returned review, without claiming autonomous judgment for the scorer code itself. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate lane state, current-lead/validation binding, reviewer role split, sealed panel reports, structural rubric gates and append-only review history. - Closure path: Producing roles&#39; claim and validation artifacts → separate reviewer access/judgment → gate objections and rejection persisted → LabDirector receives binding scientific blockers and chooses changed investigation/followup → subsequent review of revised evidence. - Boundary reachability: Gatekeepers are included in the normal agent plan and their result parsing/aggregation is wired into the production review path; this is not CI testing or a developer-only quality script. - Claim being audited: Research lead&#39;s purported novelty, technical validity, reproducibility, measured results and completeness of evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: Analyst/Runner outputs and LabDirector/Chronicle research summary. - Complementary access path: Separate gatekeeper prompts bound to the current lead and validation artifacts, literature graph and prior reviews; independent rubric outputs and objections. - Independence boundary: Distinct role/lanes, sealed review collection and prohibition on director score override, though all may use the same external model family; independence is organizational/role, not proof of model independence or external peer review. - Who acts on findings: LabDirector for scientific replanning; native gate enforces publication/promotion hold until sufficient review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-backed reviewers choose scientific objections and verdicts with their own role contracts. The JS aggregator applies those decisions but does not generate the underlying audit judgment. - Evidence: separation of powers; independent lane design; gatekeeper plan; review parsing and provenance; review objections/return. - Basis: Explicit source boundary plus executable reviewer-lane/result-return structure. - Confidence: Medium-high on first-party complementary audit mode, not on external peer-review quality. - Caveats: A Gatekeeper may see summaries rather than independently replaying an experiment; S3 positive is narrowly complementary access through separate role, literature/review evidence and scientific corrective return, not a claim of adversarial execution of the raw dataset on every run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: Model a changing scientific field and generate future research-agenda options from its beliefs, gaps and frontier, returning selected options into the current program&#39;s experimental capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New competing hypotheses and publications, oversaturated or already-solved research questions, uncertainty about future impact and which experimental directions deserve commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent-owned Scout/LabDirector field/belief/candidate synthesis and independent expert comparisons develop future-facing research options. A supported timeout mode automatically promotes the highest eligible ranked survivor; a distinct normal mode lets the researcher choose from the same evidence-grounded candidate slate, after which LabDirector operationalizes the choice. - Decision owner: Autonomous research agents own the external interpretation, slate and expert judgments in the base mode; the legitimate cycle-owning human explicitly owns the final agenda choice in the supported selection mode. Rust gate merely enforces the chosen candidate and resumes work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Field landscape quality gate, SOTA cards, instrument-blind candidate slate, sealed expert panel, novelty refutation, feasibility anchors, owner pause/selection file and timeout policy. - Closure path: Field literature/frontier → SOTA/beliefs → agent-created candidate futures → panel and novelty/feasibility filtering → automatic survivor or parent-selected candidate → LabDirector lead and reproduction anchor → current Analyst/Engineer/Runner work changes. - Boundary reachability: Expert selection is dispatched by the first-party native program when a lead is absent and its landscape/cards are ready; the published owner pause and timeout are supported runtime paths, not research-paper suggestions. - External distinction: Newly examined scientific claims, field-wide paper frontiers, contradictions, open problems and external assets in the requested research field. - Future / prospective distinction: A ranked set of not-yet-undertaken research programs designed to change accepted beliefs, evaluated for novelty, uncertainty and feasible reproduction. - Adaptation option generated: Alternative research hypotheses and experiment programs, including cross-field/bottleneck paths; the selected candidate changes the research lab&#39;s lead and required future experiment capabilities. - Path back into current capability / S3: The selected-vetted candidate is supplied as the bound new lead and next reproduction work in the director&#39;s current-control path, constraining subsequent role task creation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent roles make the field/option judgments, with an operational automatic option; separately the user has a genuine alternative supported decision right, so (P) is not inferred merely from a configuration knob. - Evidence: source landscape selection engagement; instrument-blind slate; owner choice return to lead; runtime owner checkpoint; timeout selection. - Basis: Structural source paths for field-informed future agenda and two alternative supported selection owners. - Confidence: Medium for S4 ownership boundary; high for the existence of selection paths. - Caveats: This is prospective adaptation of a research program&#39;s agenda, not proven autonomous redesign of AutoResearchLab&#39;s own software, model weights or deployment. Whether the optional timeout is enabled is configuration-dependent; no live literature search or model evaluation was run.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No separately established first-party ultimate identity/policy arbitration with a closed decision-return path at the research-program recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User research directions and binding owner directives affect venue, method count, project preferences and evidentiary discipline, but these are program/task priorities and source-quality constraints rather than a demonstrated ultimate-policy dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The owner may submit a research request, select the lead, and record binding instructions with reasons. No shipped role or explicit parent path adjudicates a conflict over the institution&#39;s identity/ultimate policy as distinct from research-agenda/current-control decisions. - Decision owner: External research owner supplies task/domain/program preferences; validity controls enforce them without acquiring ultimate-policy discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Research request/configuration, owner-directive file, role prompt injection, scientific score rules and selected-candidate enforcement. - Closure path: Owner research instructions → role task constraints and agenda decisions. Missing identity-level issue → legitimate policy deliberation/decision → governing policy returned to subsequent program operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A binding preference and research agenda selection are material parent decisions, but not automatically S5. A static instruction or scientific gate also cannot own ultimate policy merely by enforcement. - Evidence: owner directives and scope; binding role clause; owner research selection. - Basis: Bounded source-first negative after distinguishing parent agenda and scientific control from true ultimate-policy authority. - Confidence: Medium-high within the supported single research-program boundary. - Caveats: A human university or research institution can exercise a higher-recursion S5 outside the software; no organizational parent mode is imported from its mere possible presence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8610c1721397b1fd22b2b433c18fbc1e01311151/assessments/au/autoresearchlab.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8610c1721397b1fd22b2b433c18fbc1e01311151\">8610c17 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T21:32:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/VellocLabs/velloc-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Velloc Runtime</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/VellocLabs/velloc-runtime/tree/28bccb9a03b696efc1890c4b12cb1366646d975c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 28bccb9a03b696efc1890c4b12cb1366646d975c\">28bccb9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute coding/user tasks by selecting tools, editing artifacts and reacting to tool, model and delegated results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing source state, task ambiguity, model/tool errors, missing context, time and token budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose action/tool, agent delegation, implementation revision or final result. - Decision owner: Active session model; child or teammate models own their bounded delegated task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Turn reducer/interpreter, tool contracts, permission gates, model drivers, session ledger, contexts and host capabilities. - Closure path: Conversation and repository context → model request → tool/subagent decision → executed effect and observation → next turn or output. - Boundary reachability: The host loads the first-party runtime, whose session worker invokes the bundled engine; named built-in Explore, Plan and general-purpose agent routes are compiled into the shipped tool catalog. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-generated coding and tool decisions respond to execution feedback; the deterministic engine only transports and enforces permissions. - Evidence: runtime overview, turn engine, built-in agents, tool registry. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Frozen source inspection only; no live host/model session was executed by this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate collisions and incompatible sequencing between concurrently working code agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple workers changing overlapping files or acting before prerequisites complete, stale tasks and ambiguous ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Split nonoverlapping scopes, assign/claim dependency-unblocked tasks, isolate writable worktrees and return conflicts for revised allocation. - Decision owner: Team lead model following first-party agent-teams/batch protocols, with teammates updating board ownership and completion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskCreate/TaskUpdate/TaskList, SendMessage, task blocks/blockedBy, long-lived teammates, worktree isolation and per-member locks/IDs. - Closure path: Lead defines slices/dependencies → owner claims eligible work → agents execute in allocated locations → board/reports expose blocked/completed or overlapping work → lead updates assignment or integration plan. - Boundary reachability: The shipped agent-teams hosted skill unlocks team tools, while the bundled /batch skill is registered by the default stock-skill installer for compatible sessions; neither requires a third-party coordinator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead and members select scope and assignments using live shared task state. The registry stores decisions but does not autonomously decide them. - Distinct S1 units: Lead and separately running teammate/Agent worker models, each executing independent assigned code work in its own session/worktree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent writers can collide in the same source tree; a teammate may take work blocked by another; unowned or stale tasks create duplicated work. - Attenuating coordination relation: Shared owner/dependency board plus scoped worktree assignment and lead instruction to avoid overlapping slices; one worker claims eligible unblocked tasks, others take a different unit. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Task completion, ownership and blockers are returned through TaskList and mailbox reports, allowing the lead/teammates to claim the next unblocked job or revise work assignments. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It actively regulates conflicting edit scope and work eligibility between multiple independently acting S1 units, using ownership and isolated write locations with returned task-status feedback. - Evidence: agent teams instruction and gate, team board state, model-facing task tools, worktree-based batch. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: S2 is credited to user-activated team/stock-skill modes; no implicit inter-session management across every Velloc chat is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current multi-worker commitment, execution progress and integration against the common coding goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker failure, unowned or blocked tasks, results that fail validation, privilege exposure and integration readiness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead chooses next assignments, requests fixes, halts unsafe work and accepts/integrates results based on task-board and current artifact evidence. - Decision owner: Team lead model in bundled team/batch/safe-team modes, distinct from delegated implementation models. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team roster, mutable task board, automatic final-report/message turns, Shutdown, host tool permission gates, stable candidate snapshots and lead-owned validation. - Closure path: Team&#39;s live task/output state → lead assesses current readiness/security → changes assignments, requests corrections or stops the worker → updated work state returns for acceptance. - Boundary reachability: agent-teams exposes tools for lead and worker progress updates; bundled /safe-team instructs a trusted lead to review a frozen candidate and route actual validation failures back to teammates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The whole-task lead exercises discretionary management on a multi-worker whole rather than merely following a fixed per-tool policy. - Whole-system current view: The lead reads the team&#39;s complete task list, owners/dependencies, teammate status and current candidate artifacts/validation output. - Current-control decision scope: The lead can adjust assignments/priorities, request fresh fixes, stop unsafe writers, hold integration and authorize acceptance of the reviewed result for the current team task. - Evidence: team skill, task/teammate tools, safe-team current management, teammate reporting. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is conditional team-level S3, not evidence of central capacity management across all installed users/sessions or proof of real-world success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently examine a code change for actual defects and return findings for author repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Developer self-confirmation, missed reuse/design/performance defects and unsafe changes from untrusted workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Fresh reviewer agents report specific file/line faults; the author/lead triages and fixes or rejects them based on observed code. - Decision owner: Three separately spawned review-model actors in bundled simplify mode; in safe-team a trusted lead retains independent snapshot/security acceptance rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Captured git diff, independent reviewer briefs for reuse/design/performance, read-only reviewer roles, source-level findings, corrections and stable-snapshot inspection. - Closure path: Author&#39;s changed diff → separately briefed reviewer agents inspect raw code/neighbours → file/line findings → author merges/deduplicates and edits fixes → resulting artifact is returned to normal task validation. - Boundary reachability: installStockSkills registers simplify by default for compatible agent/code sessions with the native Agent tool; team-only safe-team is another shipped optional complementary path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Review models independently examine the current diff and issue discretionary findings, not merely restate the author&#39;s report or run deterministic tests. - Claim being audited: Change quality and safety against the task and existing project code. - Ordinary reporting path: The implementing model&#39;s changes and own summary. - Complementary access path: Fresh reviewers read raw git diff and surrounding repository implementation, with distinct review briefs. - Independence boundary: Separate spawned reviewer model sessions/roles from author execution; same underlying provider may be used; no independent institutional auditor claimed. - Who acts on findings: Original author model fixes findings, drops unsupported ones with reasons, and validates the result; trusted lead approves or halts untrusted code acceptance in safe-team mode. - Evidence: bundled simplify review loop, stock skill wiring, safe-team independent check, native agent dispatch. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: User-invoked or model-invoked skill mode, not mandatory audit of every change; review accuracy or an enforced second audit round is not asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No organization-level outside/future intelligence and capability adaptation loop demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Evolving external technology and future organizational fitness demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separately established prospective option authority; user or developer installs plugins and selects models. - Decision owner: No qualifying S4 agent identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scheduled routines, cross-session memory extraction/consolidation, model profile switching, extension/plugin marketplaces and developer-facing updating tools. - Closure path: Memory can be recalled and routines scheduled; no clearly owned external-future sensing → strategic option → implemented capability-change loop is returned to the task system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reorganizing prior session notes and user-configured provider changes support continuity and current tasks, not prospective environment-driven adaptation on their own. - Evidence: memory consolidation, routine scheduler, runtime overview. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A user-written task could ask an agent to research future plans, but that is not a separately shipped governance mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/legitimacy dispute arbitration and governing return established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting ultimate goals, authority claims and definition of permissible system identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No governing S5 final decision process observed within the runtime boundary. - Decision owner: Operator/host chooses capabilities, model trust and permissions; team lead controls task security, not ultimate constitutional authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Host-granted versioned capabilities, permission modes, protected paths, managed-over-user mod tiers and provider trust settings. - Closure path: Static operator policy is enforced by permission checks; no identity dispute is escalated to final authority and returned as a redefined governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safe-team is security/current-work supervision; an app-level permission envelope and developer governance are not an operational S5 function by mere presence. - Evidence: runtime capability boundary, safe-team, team provider trust. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: No parent organizational constitution or owner-level ultimate-policy decision should be inferred from default safety settings.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/67f1f884467852ba81d9cbcd153c79c85cd89666/assessments/ve/velloc-runtime.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 67f1f884467852ba81d9cbcd153c79c85cd89666\">67f1f88 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T17:04:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/roloVibes/Halo-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Halo Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/roloVibes/Halo-Harness/tree/710dde651035be3f204598aee90a1e4b85e3d6e8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 710dde651035be3f204598aee90a1e4b85e3d6e8\">710dde6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute model-directed workspace tasks and operational worker lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain code/research work, tool failures, provider/context limits and incomplete artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next tool, code/research action or response from feedback. - Decision owner: The first-party session/worker model actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Python model/tool loop, provider transports, permission engine, local tools/MCP, sessions, role lineups and worktrees. - Closure path: Model request → selected tools → observations/session state → subsequent model step or worker result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Halo owns its loop rather than merely configuring Claude Code. Claude-compatible settings and external model endpoints do not donate control functions. - Evidence: haloharness/agent/loop.py; haloharness/agent/subagent.py; haloharness/orchestra/workers.py; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Normal halo CLI/TUI and Orchestra adapters instantiate the native session/worker loop; installed role/model configuration does not require a third-party agent runtime.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Regulate overlapping file-unit writes and merge conflicts across distinct Orchestra worker lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agents may claim overlapping functions/file regions or produce mutually conflicting changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Workers choose a different claim, wait or escalate on refusal; a fresh judge selects a conflict resolution when branches collide. - Decision owner: Worker/lead model actors and the fresh conflict-judge model own the discretionary choices; code owns atomic admission and merge/test enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AST/heading/file-unit claim keys, exclusive claim log, guarded writes, per-lane worktrees, serialized merges, test checks and bounded send-back. - Closure path: Claim refusal returns holder/expiry to worker → changed claim/wait/escalation; conflicting branches → fresh judge sees goal/hunks → proposed text applied/tested → failed resolution undone and piece returned to the worker for revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A is grounded in model-owned response to a concrete cross-S1 disturbance, particularly the fresh conflict resolution and worker correction, not in deterministic locks or sequence alone. - Evidence: docs/ORCHESTRA.md; haloharness/orchestra/claims.py; haloharness/tools/orchestraclaims.py; haloharness/orchestra/merge.py; haloharness/orchestra/adapters.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Orchestra run creates real native worker sessions and exposes claim tools/guard in their run environment; merge uses the shipped fresh-judge adapter.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate whole-run current resources and commitments after forecast/return/block/stall drift. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Projected overspend, repeated returns, blocked dependencies/claims and stalled lanes threaten the current goal organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose revised model/bio, split remaining work, add a read-only specialist, retry with corrective context or stop; in review mode the owner decides whether to apply the proposed revision. - Decision owner: Auto mode: the lead model owns the checked replan. Review/parked budget mode: the legitimate local operator owns the returned acceptance/cap decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Drift thresholds, current lane/spend/forecast snapshots, plan validation, immutable merged-lane protection, pause/checkpoint, durable decision rows and deterministic apply/resume. - Closure path: Drift → lanes pause → lead receives whole remaining plan/current evidence → auto applies a valid agent plan, or review parks its diff → operator approves/rejects/adjusts cap → split/claims/workflow/forecast updated and remaining operation resumes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead makes the substantive current-control revision in auto mode; validation and budgets enforce it. Review mode separately returns an operator decision into operation. Neither hard stops nor initial task splitting alone supplies the positive witness. - Evidence: docs/SUPERVISE.md; haloharness/supervise/drift.py; haloharness/supervise/replanask.py; haloharness/supervise/replanapply.py; haloharness/supervise/decisions.py; haloharness/supervise/apply.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Optional supervised modes; replan limits/cooldown apply, completed lanes are pinned, workflow-pinned stage sets disable free replanning, and budget ceilings remain separately operator-owned.. - Boundary reachability: halo supervise/goal options expose replan:auto and replan:review; ordinary runtime adapters call the lead and apply checked plans. Attention answers are polled and applied to the same queued goal.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complement production claims with raw-code multi-dimension review, independent finding adjudication and correction/re-verification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Author summaries or reviewer allegations may be wrong; a claimed fix may leave the defect intact. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer finds code defects; independent judge confirms/dismisses against source; confirmed findings are corrected by a separate fixer and judged again against the new code. - Decision owner: Fresh read-only reviewer/judge model actors own audit judgments; a coder actor owns scoped correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Built-in dimension prompts, fixed target/diff resolution, read-only agent specs omitting CLAUDE.md, structured verdicts, serial fixes, file hashes and current-source re-verification. - Closure path: halo review target --fix → raw artifact review → judge traces claim → CONFIRMED finding goes to fixer → changed file hash/current diff → judge rechecks → fixed only when the defect is dismissed; failed/unparseable checks remain PLAUSIBLE/skipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The separate code-inspecting actors add complementary reality access and a shipped corrective return. Deterministic Orchestra gates and model tuning statistics are not promoted to audit ownership. - Evidence: docs/REVIEW.md; haloharness/review/run.py; haloharness/review/agents.py; haloharness/review/verify.py; haloharness/review/fix.py; haloharness/review/prompts/judge.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Report-only mode alone is not claimed to complete correction. The positive witness includes --fix with verification; review exit code 0 is not evidence of zero findings or approval.. - Boundary reachability: halo review and /review call the common built-in runreview; --fix is a shipped option, not an unwired developer callback, and it requires verification.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Develop prospective model-roster capability adaptations from external ecosystem options and local operating constraints, with operator selection returned into the roster/roles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing embedding/vision/role capabilities, model-family blind-spot overlap and newly available model families can leave future tasks unsupported or costly. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose whether to add/verify a new external model, retire an overlap or repoint a role after inspecting capability/cost/fit options. - Decision owner: The legitimate self-hosting operator; deterministic adviser/battery/UI supply options and enforcement, not an autonomous S4 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: External Ollama/Hugging Face library/catalog metadata and dated snapshot, local host memory/capability profiles, gap/freshness/mismatch rules, ranked options with download/VRAM/role impact, download-and-verify job and explicit role acceptance. - Closure path: External/local roster distinctions → bounded ranked adaptation options → operator selects Download and verify/retire/point → model pulled, battery profile written and role offered → accepted assignment updates the active lineup/role for subsequent work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: P is supported by a function-specific compare/select/apply path. Neither deterministic recommendation nor ordinary current model routing is called autonomous S4; the operator owns the prospective capability choice. - Evidence: docs/MODELS-adviser.md; docs/MODELS-ollama.md; haloharness/modelslibrary/advise.py; haloharness/modelslibrary/adviserules.py; haloharness/modelslibrary/browse.py; haloharness/studio/localsearchapi.py; haloharness/studio/localverifyjob.py; haloharness/studio/rolespointapi.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: The adviser is deterministic and the packaged ecosystem snapshot can be stale; freshness is not measured superiority. No live download, benchmark or successful capability gain is claimed by this source assessment.. - Boundary reachability: Official halo local advise/Studio Local Models/Library exposes suggestions and the same supported verify/assign/role-point routes; this is installed product behavior, not a research plan.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy arbitration function is supplied at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Goal stop criteria, permissions, fleet control grants, role bios and deployment policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific identity/ultimate-policy decisive return path established. - Decision owner: Operator/configuration owns constraints; no qualifying S5 owner/path established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WHAT/DONE/STOP goal fields, role/lineup configuration, approval and fleet control levels, supervised plan/budget decisions and local settings. - Closure path: Ordinary goal/resource/permission decisions return into work, but no distinct identity-level arbitration/return is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These paths close S1/S3 decisions or security constraints. Renaming them policy or giving a user final operational say does not establish S5. - Evidence: docs/ORCHESTRA.md; docs/SUPERVISE.md; docs/FLEET.md; docs/TEAM.md; haloharness/permissions.py; haloharness/supervise/decisions.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9f6ad0072b8a1021a3d4bb4675ec159f4d8488d4/assessments/ha/halo-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9f6ad0072b8a1021a3d4bb4675ec159f4d8488d4\">9f6ad00 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T19:48:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davejj1530/openorc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenOrc</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davejj1530/openorc/tree/85bf1c5e4c81000911d8a5a90e23dbb7cf869283\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 85bf1c5e4c81000911d8a5a90e23dbb7cf869283\">85bf1c5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce software/task results with durable first-party role/action/feedback composition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous tasks, code/tool failures, delegation outcomes and changing user direction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model members choose edits, delegated work, messages and terminal results through scoped MCP tools. - Decision owner: installed Codex/Claude/OpenCode actors explicitly assembled into first-party run/team roles; external CLI internals are not separately credited. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, RunHandle, task briefs, MCP scope, worktrees, snapshots, journals and settlement/recovery. - Closure path: user/task brief → provider model action → local tools/files/child result → resumed model turn → captured result and reported completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: substantive work choices come from model actors; host state transitions only transport/enforce them. - Caveats: requires installed authenticated CLI; source topology is not live model quality. - Boundary reachability: desktop/core constructs provider adapters and the scoped MCP server; team instructions are injected into actual actor turns, whose tool calls mutate the same live journal and return results. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/run-launch.ts; packages/core/src/services/run-turns.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-instructions.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-assignments.ts; packages/core/src/openorc.ts. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate shared-file and assignment interference through agent-owned claim negotiation and returned correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent coding members may edit the same shared file, duplicate ownership or apply incompatible user direction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: members choose file ownership/release, alternative work or directed negotiation; lead/members relay concrete corrections to affected workers. - Decision owner: model-backed team members/lead own coordination choices; journal claims and refusal are enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: teamclaim, teamsay/teammessage, active claims, role boundaries, captured changed files and isolated assignment integration. - Closure path: claim conflict returns holder/file → model asks colleague or selects other work → colleague releases/revises scope → later claim/work proceeds; affected active workers receive new direction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the conflict response; a fixed refusal alone is not the credited autonomous owner. - Caveats: claims are advisory and depend on model compliance; not an OS-level write sandbox, and live effectiveness is unmeasured. - Boundary reachability: Beta team execution exposes teamclaim and chat tools to running members, with conflict text returned directly to the requesting model. - Distinct S1 units: distinct coding members/contribution actors producing changes in a common project workspace or isolated child assignments. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping file ownership and incompatible concurrent edits/direction, explicitly addressed by first-party shared-workspace instructions and held-path refusal. - Attenuating coordination relation: claim before editing; on collision ask current holder in chat or choose another file; release claims and deliver scoped corrections to affected members. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: holder/conflict data and chat are included in subsequent actor turns, changing who edits which files and under which direction. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: this witness names the shared-file collision, actor-selected response and resumed work; the generic message bus and hierarchy alone are not counted. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/team-instructions.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-chat.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-delivery.ts; packages/core/src/mcp-host/team.ts. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent current-control over the whole active local team execution and its operational commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active assignments may need stopping/recovery, changed direction or intervention after conflicting/failed integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator inspects whole-team runtime and selects scoped direction, stop, actor retry, Plan/Act implementation and retained integration recovery. - Decision owner: legitimate local desktop user owns this supervisory current-control mode; lead performs delegated work coordination; fixed scheduler does not own resource discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime actor/attempt/task view, ownership checks, direction/admission keys, scoped delivery, stop/retry and guarded recovery receipts. - Closure path: whole-team runtime → operator intervention/direction → coordinator fences/stops/retries or delivers changed direction → actors resume or withhold work under the returned decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: P is based on the combined team-wide view and applied intervention path, not a generic stop button or the class name TeamCoordinator. No autonomous whole-system S3 choice is established. - Caveats: scope is one local team/project execution, not all organizations or a global fleet. User must exercise the supported control; source establishes capability, not observed intervention history. - Boundary reachability: desktop orchestration RPC exposes current team runtime and guarded send/stop/retry/implementPlan/recovery methods; they act on the owned execution journal rather than an adjacent dashboard. - Whole-system current view: team conversation runtime includes pinned team revision, execution actors/attempts, retained work/results and recovery state across active member cells. - Current-control decision scope: current team commitment/direction, withholding or restarting affected actors, moving planning to implementation and applying retained integration recovery; scheduler limits only enforce the selected envelope. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/team-conversation.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-coordinator.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-attempt-recovery.ts; packages/core/src/handlers/orchestration.ts. - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independent source-backed model review challenges proposed code before user publication or corrective implementation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported completion claims and defects in proposed PR code; previous findings may remain after revision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate review model chooses findings and summary against raw checkout/diff; user retains posting and follow-through selection. - Decision owner: dedicated review conversation/model owns audit judgment; Git checkout/draft parser and human publication are distinct support/control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: detached PR checkout, Plan-mode review, scoped finding/summary tools, exact-commit drafts and re-review after head change. - Closure path: raw PR code/diff → separate reviewer model findings → draft visible to operator/control; selected findings can be sent into task/team implementation and later review checks whether addressed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: audit judgment is model-authored, not the coder completion narrative or deterministic Git diff. Human ownership of external posting does not author the audit conclusion. - Caveats: not independent provider validation or measured detection efficacy; arbitrary team member named reviewer alone is not credited. - Boundary reachability: the shipped PR-review service creates the dedicated conversation/checkout, injects review-only instructions and exposes draft finding tools; normal task/team review selections have first-party return paths. - Claim being audited: proposed source changes are correct and prior findings have been addressed. - Ordinary reporting path: implementation conversation, task result and PR description. - Complementary access path: detached current-head source checkout plus direct diff, with subsequent reviews comparing changed commits rather than merely repeating author reports. - Independence boundary: separate review conversation in Plan mode with explicit prohibition on implementation, file changes, push and posting; isolated from the author workspace. - Who acts on findings: operator sees/selects draft findings; task/team review-send paths return selected corrections to implementation; subsequent model review checks retained findings against new code. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/pull-requests.ts; packages/core/src/services/pull-request-checkout.ts; packages/core/src/services/review.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-tasks.ts. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no outside-and-future adaptation loop into OpenOrc current capability established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new task context, project lessons, user preferences and updated software versions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: memory extraction and instruction changes retain collaboration context; updater checks releases. - Decision owner: task/Orcling agents own local content changes; operator and maintainers own externally selected capability/product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project memory/retrieval, Orcling histories/instructions, model selection and release updater. - Closure path: past work/user preference is reused or a released app is updated; no prospective environmental model develops adaptation options and returns a capability decision into current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory, self-instruction edits and update notification do not by themselves satisfy S4. - Caveats: does not rule out a larger organization using outputs for strategy. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/memory.ts; packages/core/src/services/orcling-briefs.ts; packages/core/src/services/orclings.ts; apps/desktop/src/main/app-updates.ts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain a persistent Orcling behavioral identity/charter in alternative agent-owned and parent-governed modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new lasting collaboration requirements or owner dissatisfaction require changing the same actor instructions across future sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allow mode lets the Orcling choose and save revised enduring instructions; Approve/Plan requests explicit parent authorization, and user profile editing/restoration supplies a direct parent mode. - Decision owner: Orcling model for autonomous instruction revision; local user for approved/editor/restore mode and immutable permission/identity ceilings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned orclinginstructions, reason/author records, action-policy approval gate, profile editing/restoration and fresh-process identity brief. - Closure path: lasting identity/working rule → model revised charter or user-selected edit/restore → version saved → next session/process injects current instructions wherever the actor works. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the autonomous base is the local actor charter, not authority to widen host permissions; parent mode separately owns identity/permission selection and approved charter change. - Caveats: not every instruction edit is S5: the positive witness concerns persistent identity/behavioral mandate. Models remain under parent-set host permission ceilings; no global institutional S5 is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Orcling tools are mounted for the saved speaker; updateInstructions authorizes according to its effective mode and saves a version. New provider processes receive the same saved identity/current charter through orclingBrief. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the long-term companion role and enduring behavioral instructions that define how this actor works across projects and conversations. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Allow mode delegates charter revision to the actor within its immutable host ceiling; Approve/Plan and user edit/restore retain parent decision over the proposed charter/identity. - Return-to-operation path: saveInstructions/restoreInstructions append the authoritative version; orclingBrief puts current instructions into each newly started process, with an explicit next-session boundary. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/orcling-briefs.ts; packages/core/src/services/orclings.ts; packages/core/src/services/app-actions.ts; packages/core/src/services/run-approvals.ts; packages/core/src/mcp-host/orcling.ts; docs/orclings.md. - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a4215303042bfc9da6b6d818d0713d0fe9f20001/assessments/op/openorc.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a4215303042bfc9da6b6d818d0713d0fe9f20001\">a421530 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T12:53:31Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maximarhipkin/ferrule\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ferrule</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maximarhipkin/ferrule/tree/24023deffa325405db3f260a5a03d736976fa1b0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 24023deffa325405db3f260a5a03d736976fa1b0\">24023de &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute coding/assistant/scheduled outcomes through a model/tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific files, commands, service responses, tool failures and verification errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select actions, tool arguments and continuation after observations. - Decision owner: root and child model-backed agents within their assigned outcomes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, tool registry, sandbox/credential proxy, verification, transcript, scheduler and budget/stuck guards. - Closure path: call → tools → observations → repeated model choice; returned results and failed check tails enter subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: shipped run/chat/gateway and child factories construct ferrulecore::Agent and equip product tools; no external cognitive harness is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: enforcement alone cannot choose the next task-dependent action if the model is removed. - Evidence: shipped runtime; core loop entry; action/verification loop; child construction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent duplicate task effort and mutually destructive sibling workspace edits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two children attempt the same work or modify the same parent checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/claim a non-conflicting task, select worktree isolation and decide when to integrate a returned branch. - Decision owner: participating model agents, with the root owning integration into its checkout. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: atomic SQLite claims/dependency refusals, isolated worktrees and returned branch/workspace notes. - Closure path: claim refusal returns current ownership/dependency information; agents select other work or wait; worker changes remain isolated until root merge/cherry-pick. - Boundary reachability: the supplied tasks and spawnagent tools are registered for the actual running tree, not hypothetical external coordination code. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent root/worker/planner agents producing separate code/work outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate task claims and concurrent code edits contaminating one another. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive task claim plus per-worker worktree and root-controlled integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: claim/result/branch metadata changes what work an agent can take and which changes enter subsequent parent operation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: atomic task ownership and workspace separation directly regulate identified duplicate-effort/edit collisions; the shared board alone is not the witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents choose tasks/isolation and root integration; transactions/git merely enforce those choices. - Evidence: task choice/feedback contract; atomic conflict-aware claim; tool dispatch; isolation wiring; worktree integration boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Outside git or on worktree failure a child may share the workspace; this is reported, not claimed equally isolated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current child commitments/capacity and deployment spending on behalf of the root tree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: too many live commitments, shared token scarcity and children needing intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root selects wait/message/stop and renewed child effort; parent selects shared caps and approved administrative intervention. - Decision owner: root agent in the base tree-control mode; legitimate self-hosting operator in the separate parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child status/token projection, budget warnings, stop flags/tree accounting, admin approval cards, shared Hub caps and audit/config persistence. - Closure path: root interventions change running child behaviour or stop descendants; approved parent cap changes are applied to the live Hub and subsequent runs are governed by them. - Boundary reachability: messageagent/stopagent are actual root tools; owner chat ferruleadmin and dashboard use the same first-party control APIs. No adjacent benchmark supervisor is credited. - Whole-system current view: root lists its current children with role/status/token use and reports, and receives tree budget feedback; operator sees gateway health/current turns, tasks and shared caps. - Current-control decision scope: current operational commitments and scarce tree/deployment resources, beyond merely decomposing the user&#39;s task. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root chooses intervention under current operational feedback; fixed max-depth/token checks do not own that choice. In parent mode the operator&#39;s exact approval/cap decision owns the intervention. - Evidence: current child view and intervention tools; current consumption view; tree budget feedback; parent health/current control; operator status view; live cap return; approval execution. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A is scoped to root-tree current commitments; it is not an autonomous governor of all gateway roots or OSS governance. The P mode is independently supported, not simultaneous dual ownership.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: optionally challenge a worker/parent claim or code change through a separate verifier&#39;s raw-artifact access. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a plausible completion report may hide broken tests, incorrect code or contaminated evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verifier chooses proof commands/code inspection and judges whether the submitted claim holds. - Decision owner: a non-forked verifier agent in a separate session; parent acts on returned findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifier-specific prompt/role, throwaway workspace snapshot, separate transcript, optional distinct provider and parent notice/report return. - Closure path: verifier result is delivered to the parent, which can reject the claim, redirect/restart work or inspect further through its existing message/stop/tool paths. - Boundary reachability: spawnagent role=verifier is a shipped runtime mode with role-specific placement and child construction, not a test/eval-only role. - Claim being audited: a submitted operational completion claim or code change. - Ordinary reporting path: worker final report/status to parent. - Complementary access path: verifier directly reads the copied code including uncommitted/new files and runs proof commands. - Independence boundary: separate non-forked model context and snapshot; verifier does not implement/repair production changes. Same provider is allowed but is not claimed as model independence. - Who acts on findings: the root/parent with authority to redirect or stop the relevant child and change its own next operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier model owns the evidence-selection/verdict, rather than a deterministic parser or self-reported success flag. - Evidence: audit role purpose; snapshot placement; fresh child context/role; return and parent response modes; verifier workspace boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional claim-relative audit mode, not every verifier invocation. Forking producer context, using it as ordinary same-path QA, or choosing non-isolated shared work can invalidate this independence claim; no live audit or general reliability guarantee is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied outside-and-future adaptation process with the required supervisory coupling established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future external provider/tool/user changes could demand capability adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped future-relevant option judgment and two-way S3 adoption loop established. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: offline reflection/probing/playbook deltas, model discovery/testing/fallbacks, doctor and install/update/admin operations. - Closure path: learning applies lessons to later prompts and admin changes current capability; the inspected triggers are retrospective internal failures or present configuration needs, without an explicit external/prospective adaptation distinction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: learning between runs and autonomous self-configuration are not sufficient S4 evidence. - Evidence: retrospective learning inputs; learning implementation; contrastive pass scope; admin change scope. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain legitimate ultimate authority over the deployment&#39;s autonomy/security envelope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: whether Ferrule may act without per-change consent while protected keys and owner-only boundaries remain reserved. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the operator chooses Ask me first versus Protected autonomous and controls security settings that the bot cannot alter. - Decision owner: the authenticated deployment owner via the dashboard; the model cannot grant itself this authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: page-only sandbox-mode API, confirmation/fresh-config guard, protected configuration keys, audit record, saved policy and gateway restart. - Closure path: the owner&#39;s mode choice is saved and audited; the gateway restarts with the returned sandbox/trust envelope, changing subsequent admin/tool behaviour. - Boundary reachability: dashboard sandboxmode is a first-party operating endpoint explicitly omitted from chat operation routing; owner-mode and supervised admin prompts/tools are wired alternatives. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the system&#39;s standing autonomy/delegation and protected-secret authority boundary, rather than approval of a single task. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: deployment owner; autonomous admin operation inside the envelope does not authorize changing the envelope itself. - Return-to-operation path: confirmed owner choice → policy/config save → gateway restart → subsequent operation under chosen consent/autonomy mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may perform permitted admin work, but cannot decide its own ultimate sandbox/delegation policy. - Evidence: owner-only envelope and protected limits; actual authoritative switch and return; distinct subsequent admin behaviour; chat routes omit sandbox authority. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Linux owner mode has platform-specific constraints. This is deployment-parent policy, not a claim of project-level governance or that ordinary approval cards alone are S5.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ea30365da43e2bcde1d7bcb779bed890626be1d6/assessments/fe/ferrule.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ea30365da43e2bcde1d7bcb779bed890626be1d6\">ea30365 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T21:13:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davesheffer/coding-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Coding Orchestrator</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davesheffer/coding-orchestrator/tree/dca682d7a1bc99c6b83508ad03186b2015085e8a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dca682d7a1bc99c6b83508ad03186b2015085e8a\">dca682d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous coding/research/test work by the host-model agent and installed native scout/runner/builder agents with bounded task/tool loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain repository structure, source changes, failing checks, implementation alternatives and task-local tool feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main session model designs/solves ambiguous work, the scout selects what to investigate within its brief, the builder chooses concrete edits/validation for a specified change, and the runner interprets exact check outcome without changing the task&#39;s scope. - Decision owner: Claude Code/Codex main-session and named-child models reachable through installed first-party role contracts, not the Python installer or preconfigured model strings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installed role files, native subagent invocation, explicit model/effort/brief, read-only helper/guard, sandbox checks where supported, typed outcome report and optional Windows launcher. - Closure path: user objective → main model investigation/design and bounded role assignment → child-model tools/read/check/edit feedback → RESULT/EVIDENCE/CONFIDENCE/UNVERIFIED → main model checks, corrects or completes task. - Boundary reachability: default installers put the role definitions and main charter into the selected assistant home; starting a supported Claude/Codex session exposes the named roles. The Windows launcher separately supplies a real restricted CLI fallback, while native host execution is conditional on role discovery and effective permissions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves static roles, installers, command/report helpers and sandbox policy but no task-specific decisions or coding output. The external client executes inference, while the installed first-party definitions directly shape real normal-operation model roles. - Evidence: role dispatch rules, Claude role routing, builder role, scout role, launch restrictions. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for distributed role construction; provider-authenticated live role selection and effective sandbox enforcement were not run here. - Caveats: an optional Windows launcher cannot be silently assumed to work on Linux/macOS, and mere role model metadata is weaker than observing a live session. The role pack&#39;s own validation record describes these native gaps.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: direct attenuation of overlapping file edits and order-dependent interference among several independent coding S1 subunits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent builders/writers touching one file or repository without disjoint ownership, overlapping edits and approvals during unresolved prior work, and invalid test results produced before a dependency completes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main model assigns disjoint paths and bounded briefs to independent subagents, keeps conflicting edits/dependencies sequential, and changes assignment/wait order after builder diffs, check exits and verification indicate overlap or dependency failure. - Decision owner: installed main-session orchestration model, constrained by real user scope and native permission boundaries; deterministic role safety guards are not the coordination decision maker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role separation (scout/critic read-only, runner check-only, builder scoped writing), explicit file-ownership clauses, dependency/wait rules, actual diffs and result reports, host&#39;s task completion notices, and conditional checked sandbox boundaries. - Closure path: proposed independent work may touch the same mutable code or require an earlier fix → main model gives non-overlapping ownership or delays the dependent worker → observed diff/check/report returns → main model corrects assignment or waits/replans → later model-owned S1 edits use the revised scope/order. - Boundary reachability: both installed main-session charters explicitly instruct disjoint file ownership, parallelism only for independent units, sequential edits/dependencies and checking actual child results; native named agents are reachable after installation. No unrelated external work manager is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model decides which changes are independent and how to respond to observed overlap; static role restrictions and a guard may reduce the chance of unsafe writes but cannot select a domain-specific collision resolution. - Distinct S1 units: at least two concurrently or sequentially handed-off native child models (for example separate scout/runner and builder branches, or two scoped builders) working on one coding project under the same main model. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two builders can produce conflicting edits to shared files, while a runner checking an incomplete dependency can report stale results that mislead the later builder/parent. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party parent role contract with disjoint file ownership, specified briefs and ordered dependency/editor actions, plus returned diffs/reports and correction by the main model. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the main model inspects observed builder diffs and real runner exits, then narrows or revises a worker&#39;s file scope, reassigns the fix or delays the next writer until conflicting work is reconciled. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: disjoint ownership and dependent-write/check ordering are aimed at a concrete interference among independently acting code-changing S1 roles, not merely routing routine low-cost tasks. - Evidence: Codex no-overlap/task/dependency contract, Claude task partition, builder scoped writing, Codex named role definition. - Basis: explicit skill-driven inter-writer operating policy. - Confidence: medium for model-owned S2 in correctly followed multi-role operation; no runtime global file-claim database or automatic source-collision detector exists. - Caveats: generic task delegation or read-only scout/critic isolation alone is not S2; this claim rests on distinct changing/observing S1 units and source-overlap prevention/correction through the parent model. A single writer mode does not exercise inter-S1 coordination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: whole-current project control by the main model over delegated task commitments, uncertainty, verification obligations and risk/acceptance interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing current tasks, unverified work, weak reports, elapsed context, child sandbox failures, failed tests, risk/irreversible actions and incomplete handoffs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main model chooses whether to delegate or handle directly, allocates roles/briefs, analyzes child reports/diffs and risk, requires another check/critic, changes task plan/assignment and accepts or returns work for correction. - Decision owner: installed main-session orchestration model. A real user retains authorization over pushes and irreversible actions, but no distinct parent-governed whole-current S3 mode is inferred from occasional permission approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RESULT/EVIDENCE/CONFIDENCE/UNVERIFIED reports, role constraints, verification commands, client child-task state, optional Mission Control in-flight UI, agent-report.py, relay context gauge/handoff, Jev route/guard hints and actual client permission enforcement. - Closure path: current parallel child outcomes/changed files, low-confidence reports and check exits → model main-session portfolio judgment → correction/delegation/escalation/critic/pass decision → revised child work, accepted code or bounded new session continuation. - Boundary reachability: the main-session charter is installed and loaded as the normal operating instruction; native role responses return to that session, optional UI/launcher provide further observations. Its job is explicitly design, judgment, verification and final response rather than merely waiting for a model child to finish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static rules and context thresholds advise or constrain; the host model owns what to do with the whole current request and whether evidence is sufficient. A dashboard, hook or permission check does not exercise that judgment alone. - Whole-system current view: main conversation receives each launched child result and task status, and can inspect diffs/checks; optional Mission Control displays the active model/agent timeline and report cards. The optional Windows launcher aggregates its own bounded run reports, not arbitrary independent client sessions. - Current-control decision scope: split/reassign project work, sequence disjoint edits, require direct validation or critic review, stop an unsafe course, and choose release readiness within user-granted authority. - Evidence: Codex parent-owned supervision, Claude parent-owned supervision, Mission Control current observation, agent report helper, role handoff contracts. - Basis: explicit operating ownership, supported by native role/report interfaces. - Confidence: medium-high for supported agent-caller mode. - Caveats: Claude shell restrictions may be merely advisory under inherited permissions; actual client permissions and model availability must be verified on deployment. The Mission Control mod is optional and observability, not an independent controller.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: model-owned independent challenge of risky code changes and correctness/security claims using direct source and verification evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer&#39;s unsupported confidence, a passing-but-irrelevant test, concurrency/security bugs missed by builder and main agent, and accidental self-review of an asserted fix. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately instantiated critic model with a fresh brief independently judges the exact diff/source and produces SHIP | FIX FIRST | RETHINK findings; the main model then resolves/acts on the evidence before claiming the risky work done. - Decision owner: shipped critic role running as a distinct Claude/Codex model agent for independent audit judgment; main model owns remediation and final acceptance, rather than the builder or an automatic CI lint gate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh-context adversarial role prompts, read-only policy (with effective-enforcement caveats), exact diff/base and claim, expanding through callers/error paths/tests, failure scenarios, line-anchored findings, typed verdict and main-session correction loop. - Closure path: builder/main proposes risky change or completion claim → separately briefed critic reads direct changed code and adjacent paths → independent finding/SHIP/FIX FIRST/RETHINK returns → main model verifies, requests changes or documents conscious remaining risk before acceptance. - Boundary reachability: Claude installer places agents/critic.md, Codex installer places the native critic.toml; both installed main charters explicitly require critic review for risky or irreversible work. It does not rely on a repo maintainer PR process or a hypothetical external assessor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critic model owns a substantive adversarial audit verdict from complementary direct evidence; the static role manifest/guards and main model&#39;s acceptance are separate functions. The builder&#39;s own test/report is not substituted for an independent critic. - Claim being audited: that a proposed coding change or high-risk operation is correct, safe and supported by the stated check evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: builder or runner returns RESULT/EVIDENCE/CONFIDENCE/UNVERIFIED and main reads the actual work/checks. - Complementary access path: distinct fresh-context critic receives the exact diff/base and claim, reads actual code/callers/error cases/tests without accepting author reasoning, and reports concrete adverse scenarios with file/line evidence. - Independence boundary: critic has separate role, model/prompt/brief and read-only posture, no shared author conclusions as settled truth; practical sandbox isolation depends on actual client/host and may be unverified even when role files state read-only. - Who acts on findings: the main orchestration model investigates blocker/major/minor evidence, orders corrective work, reruns checks or explicitly withholds final acceptance; an automatic critic response does not publish changes. - Evidence: Claude critic charter, Codex critic definition, fresh critic routing/feedback, Claude critic acceptance, native validation boundaries. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for distinct installed critical review path, subject to client runtime configuration. - Caveats: runtime enforcement for Claude critic may remain instruction-level under permissive inherited modes, and no paid critic ran in this assessment. Optional user refusal or unsupported native role availability may prevent a specific deployment from exercising S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installed organizational future-environment sensing → adaptation-option generation → decision → returned operating capability loop is supplied for the assessed host coding project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model tiers, provider availability, token use and future tooling change appear as configuration/routing concerns, not a functionally closed S4 strategy system. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4 owner is bound to a proactive future adaptation cycle; agent choices over current work and context rollover are S3/continuity decisions. - Decision owner: neither autonomous system-model intelligence authority nor a qualifying distinct parent-governed S4 mode is established within the installed bundle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-routing table and fallback rules, context gauge/rollover, optional Jev classification, agent-report.py, benchmarks/optimization research and human configuration updates. - Closure path: no evidenced future/external distinction → novel adaptation option → authoritative selection → returned change to current system capability. A context threshold may trigger a fresh session but preserves current objectives instead of adapting future organizational capabilities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the host model can choose a different helper or consult benchmarks, but the released bundle does not supply a strategic outside-and-then intelligence role and completed adaptation return loop. - Evidence: role/routing and continuity rules, Claude relay instructions, relay code, Jev routing, benchmarks. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: medium-high for examined installation and runtime paths. - Caveats: separate external corporate/user strategy may change the role bundle; the repo&#39;s benchmark and optimization materials are not an installed, agent-owned prospective capability governance loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party autonomous or parent-governed ultimate identity/policy decision and subsequent charter-return loop is installed for the coding system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task scope, authorization, tool/network boundaries, project requirements and model availability are externally configured and handled as task/permission governance, not identity disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user grants or withholds outward/destructive actions and any optional permission fallback; main agent respects the configured role policy, but cannot adjudicate its own ultimate identity or bind the user/project through an S5 resolution institution. - Decision owner: no legitimate S5 mode owner within the installed runtime is demonstrated. User-owned config/approval remains at an external parent but is not a full S5 function-specific return path merely because permission checks exist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installed AGENTS/CLAUDE instructions, role sandbox settings, user-owned agent-routing.json exception list, external client permissions, Jev risk gate and optional Hunch outside the actual installed managed block. - Closure path: blocked tool or permission fallback request → user/current-model task decision → resumed/denied particular operation; no defined ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authoritative verdict → binding identity charter update → subsequent operation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent following constraints has S1/S3 operating rights but no supreme authority to change the system&#39;s charter. A user can reinstall/change settings, but installing code and occasional approval are not S5 parent governance. - Evidence: Codex role permission and consent boundaries, user-local policy handling, Claude parent authority limits, installed managed-block boundary, opt-in Jev guard. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: high for no shipped identity-level return forum at declared boundary. - Caveats: independent OSS users may each govern their assistant installations; do not aggregate their private settings into an organization-wide S5 or borrow the separate Hunch system as this bundle&#39;s own installed authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/20404580837d682e46e9d185d4f7cbf08fdb4888/assessments/co/coding-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 20404580837d682e46e9d185d4f7cbf08fdb4888\">2040458 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T21:04:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CodeAlive-AI/pragmatic-orchestration\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pragmatic Orchestration</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CodeAlive-AI/pragmatic-orchestration/tree/cc829d908822978fe7fce4670967525ea3a9a267\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cc829d908822978fe7fce4670967525ea3a9a267\">cc829d9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: independent model-directed coding, codebase research and validation inside a bounded delegated task, including normal host-model task work when no external worker is needed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing source and requirements, implementation choices, test/tool feedback and partial failures within one assigned workspace and task contract. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the selected external coding-agent model chooses investigation, edits, tools, tests and substantive answer inside its assigned scope; the caller model chooses the task contract and later acceptance but does not precompute every worker action. - Decision owner: configured Codex/Claude/OpenCode/Grok/Devin model actor in each actual delegated CLI session; a supported skill-capable host model owns its own nondelegated task decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: porch delegate agent selection, backend adapters, configured model/profile, prompt transport, exact cwd, session/result artifacts, supervisor/registry, bounded status and final-result delivery; external provider models supply inference rather than Porch-owned hidden capabilities. - Closure path: host model assigns a bounded objective → backend model executes its own task/tool feedback loop → code/research/check result and evidence return through the supervisor → calling model checks/accepts or steers/reassigns unfinished work. - Boundary reachability: SKILL.md routes installed skill-capable callers to the shipped executable porch entrypoint, whose delegate dispatcher, Python supervisor and adapters genuinely start the configured worker; this is an advertised real use path, not a test-only fake agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without a model-driven worker, the wrapper can transport tasks and persist events but cannot select the task-specific actions or produce substantive code; without the caller model, the skill does not itself choose the task contract. - Evidence: installed skill delegation map, delegate launcher, supervisor and adapter invocation, delegate runtime. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for shipped model/task/runtime connection; no live provider invocation was attempted. - Caveats: the actual provider binary, account/quota and selected skill-capable host are external installation dependencies. Do not infer provider-internal multi-agent coordination as Porch S2/S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuation of conflicts between independently writing S1 agents during overlapping project delegation and corrective replacement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent writes to the same mutable checkout, a successor modifying partially written files before its predecessor is actually stopped, and different task scopes modifying each other&#39;s work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model caller gives disjoint task scopes and uses distinct user-authorized workspaces for concurrent writers; when new events/diffs expose conflict or failure, it decides whether to redirect/stop an exact worker, preserve partial changes, or withhold a replacement assignment until the writer is confirmed stopped. - Decision owner: the skill-guided calling model in the supported multi-delegate mode. The user has prior authority over workspace creation; the supervisor/registry only enforces run-addressed lifecycle and transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate user-provided working directories, explicit task scope contracts, run IDs, delegate list/events/status/cancel/steer, stop/acknowledgment verification, VCS status and diff checks, and the first-party overlapping-writer guard in delegation policy. - Closure path: two model worker tasks could contend for one checkout → model caller assigns distinct approved workspaces or keeps the replacement waiting → observes run outcomes/partial diffs → sends corrective steer or cancels and confirms terminal status → only then permits the next conflicting S1 writer to act. - Boundary reachability: the shipped skill has explicit multi-worker/parallel-writer and stop-before-reassignment instructions while porch exposes independent running worker IDs, separate cwd binding, observation and targeted intervention. This is a supported optional multiple-delegate operating arrangement, conditional on user-authorized workspaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the caller model judges actual overlap and which worker/scope to change; the private registry prevents mis-addressed control messages but does not detect or autonomously resolve file conflicts. - Distinct S1 units: at least two independently executing coding agents each with a separate model/tool loop and one assigned project task/working directory. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous unrelated writers on one checkout can overwrite, intermix or invalidate changes; premature reassignment into a predecessor&#39;s partially modified tree repeats the conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party mandatory separate-workspace/clear-scope policy plus substantive parent checkpointing, explicit stop/verify before conflicting replacement, and corrective steering into subsequent tasks. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: run event/cancellation outcome and inspected repository diff go to the model caller; it changes the next worker&#39;s root/scope, or sends the exact active worker a corrective task before continuing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the skill identifies a concrete inter-writer contention hazard and prescribes an operational anti-overlap/feedback relation among distinct worker loops, not merely a mailbox or job queue. - Evidence: delegate multi-writer and replacement contract, parent skill contract, run-addressed control commands, remote workspace guidance. - Basis: explicit operating contract backed by active run-addressed tools. - Confidence: medium: model-owned preventive and corrective protocol is shipped, but there is no automatic cross-workspace write-collision detector or proof that callers always follow the skill. - Caveats: single-delegate or manually co-located writers do not realize this S2 path. Runtime locks protect metadata/mailboxes, not shared repository writes; user must authorize separate writer workspaces.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model-caller regulation of the whole current delegated project workload and commitments through project supervision, intervention and acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled worker progress, wrong task interpretations, partial or failed outcomes, unexpected deviations, competing active work, uncertainty over delivery/steering and incomplete acceptance criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the calling model evaluates the active delegation portfolio and substantive evidence, chooses new assignments/reprioritization, corrective steering, cancellation, reassignment and whether work is accepted or requires further verification. - Decision owner: the operating skill-capable host model in its agent-orchestrator mode; CLI lifecycle commands are instruments and user scope constraints remain binding. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current run enumeration, events cursors, wait-any group snapshots, per-run complete final texts, exact steer delivery status, deviation journals, verified tests/diffs and optional remote-host status. - Closure path: several active worker status/progress/evidence streams → host model forms current project-wide judgment → targeted steer, cancel, replacement, revised workload or acceptance → subsequent worker execution/commitments and verification change. - Boundary reachability: the installed skill expressly directs the model caller to inspect each worker during its first minute, adapt later supervision, keep the parent turn active, observe several runs and make intervention/acceptance decisions; all named control/observation commands are routed to shipped CLI and supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model discretion determines current priorities, whether the work is useful, scope of corrective action and acceptance. A process exit, heartbeat, quota readout or wait-any timeout alone would not make that judgment. - Whole-system current view: delegate list --active enumerates the private run registry, while wait-any reports complete supplied-group state/timing and events/artifacts provide substantive per-worker evidence; the calling model must retain the project group membership rather than infer it from a global list. - Current-control decision scope: allocate distinct worker contracts, redirect exact active sessions, stop unproductive work, delay conflicting replacement, coordinate completion and accept/reject worker contributions against task criteria. - Evidence: model caller supervision and acceptance contract, group wait and event interfaces, registry control, parent-owned review acceptance. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for model-run supported project supervision, absent end-to-end provider session observation. - Caveats: neither the CLI nor optional read-only UI has autonomous whole-fleet scheduling, and a human issuing an occasional CLI stop is not enough to establish a separate operational parent-governed S3 mode. Periodic checks are a caller obligation, not a built-in timer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independent, direct-evidence auditing of implementation/correctness/security claims from worker work, with corrective results fed to the caller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion reports, unsafe patches, stale or invented code quotations, defects not visible in a worker&#39;s self-summary, and one actor rubber-stamping its own work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately launched read-only reviewer model assesses the actual source/diff and reports independently reasoned defects; optionally an independent judge model adjudicates combined discoveries, while the calling model chooses corrective disposition. - Decision owner: one or several review-mode model agents operating under the first-party audit prompt rather than the implementing worker&#39;s instruction/authority, followed by the caller model for remedial action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review code direct file/diff input, role-specific security/correctness and optional specialist passes, read-only review contract, per-pass independent reports, cross-check of quoted code against files, optional deterministic dedup and separate LLM judge, final report delivery. - Closure path: worker claims a change is correct/ready → independent review model directly inspects the file/diff and broadens relevant source context → source-anchored findings reach the caller → caller verifies and commands corrective work, targeted tests, or reasoned acceptance before completion. - Boundary reachability: porch review code basic is an ordinary shipped CLI mode, and review ask/super/ultra are explicit supported modes. The skill instructs the host caller to invoke reviewers separately, supply task contracts, and act on findings; no private maintainer CI reviewer is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct reviewing model agents make risk/finding judgments from independent direct access; quote checking/dedup scripts only validate or combine evidence, while the primary worker cannot rewrite those review judgments through its own completion message. - Claim being audited: a delegated coding agent&#39;s implementation/report or the caller&#39;s proposed code change satisfies task and security/correctness requirements. - Ordinary reporting path: implementing worker&#39;s session events, final answer, own tests and source changes return to its calling model through delegate wait. - Complementary access path: independent review code reader receives current source file/diff plus an explicitly incomplete seed and is required to expand its own repository search; findings carry file/line/quoted-code anchors checked against source. - Independence boundary: reviewers are separately invoked read-only model sessions with dedicated audit roles and denied edits/delegation; they are not the working delegate&#39;s self-attestation. Sharing vendor/model family or an agent-selected initial file seed does not itself guarantee diversity; optional judge is another audit pass, not substitute evidence. - Who acts on findings: the calling orchestration model verifies dispositions against the task contract and steers/assigns corrections; neither basic/specialist report nor automatic judge verdict unilaterally accepts worker changes. - Evidence: review operational mode, specialist review dispatch, quote validation, review workflow stages, task-relative remediation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for operational independence and direct-source audit; no live reviewers were paid/run at the frozen source. - Caveats: optional review must actually be invoked for a particular task; basic/specialists do not have an internal judge, and no automated merge gate forces a review finding to block acceptance. Read-only enforcement varies by backend.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supported first-party organizational loop that senses changing external/future conditions, develops strategic alternatives and returns a capability adaptation decision into the whole current system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential changes in future provider quality, coding technology, cost, environment or long-horizon deployment capability are discussed, but not closed through a shipped prospective sensing/adaptation process. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not supplied for S4; a caller can manually choose another model/provider or alter configuration, and retrospective session history can inform ad hoc choices, without a distinct future-oriented intelligence owner and returned capability-change loop. - Decision owner: no autonomous or legitimate parent S4 decision owner is established within this distribution boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/profile configuration, quota snapshots, session history stats/flags, advice to periodically inspect agent effectiveness, external-model comparison tips and remote-host provisioning helpers. - Closure path: no demonstrated outside-and-then sensing → alternative-generation → adaptation decision → changed future capability/S3 closure; ordinary task replanning and same-run steering are S3, not S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an optional model&#39;s analysis of past sessions could be extended into strategy, but the installed skill does not bind that analysis to first-party prospective adaptation authority and returned operating-capability change. - Evidence: skill review/delegation modes, session-history tools, configuration and quota, remote-host options. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: medium-high within current shipped operational paths. - Caveats: a user-built future-planning program or another organization&#39;s S4 could consume Porch telemetry, but is not a supplied first-party S4 constructor merely because telemetry exists.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity-level or ultimate-policy adjudication mechanism with legitimate decisive ownership and returned governance of Porch operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing permissible operating boundaries, provider identities, security exposure and project goals are limited by human-owned installation/configuration/task contracts, not resolved by a Porch ultimate-policy institution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: caller/user chooses task scope, external agent accounts, writable roots and provider configuration; per-run permission errors and acceptance decisions do not become identity-level policy arbitration. - Decision owner: no supported autonomous S5 owner or closed parent-governed S5 mode for the whole assessed system is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review read-only prompt, role/tool restrictions where provider supports them, external credentials/config, remote host security hardening, user authorization before parallel writer workspaces and ordinary acceptance criteria. - Closure path: provider permission refusal or task-scope concern → caller/user decision over that task; no shipped identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate top-level arbitration → authoritative update → subsequent Porch charter enforcement loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Porch has operational guardrails and user-set credentials/roots; a model applying those constraints does not author its own ultimate identity, and the parent can configure the tool without performing a defined S5 return loop. - Evidence: skill operating limits, delegate authorization/workspace contract, configuration, remote host security. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: high for absence of a defined identity authority path among inspected shipped runtime and policy surfaces. - Caveats: maintainers and users can revise the published skill, but contribution governance and configuration change are not a first-party operational S5 closure at this system recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ca22ec1154f38dc3128c0d2258d453ee0ab2644c/assessments/pr/pragmatic-orchestration.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ca22ec1154f38dc3128c0d2258d453ee0ab2644c\">ca22ec1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T16:55:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Aeven-AI/Aeven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aeven</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Aeven-AI/Aeven/tree/50e27b33594b7f0400fc666668fc1026c3fe7ca1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 50e27b33594b7f0400fc666668fc1026c3fe7ca1\">50e27b3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry user tasks through first-party model/tool and continuable child work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user objectives, tool failures, incomplete artifacts, interruptions and missing context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next substantive tool/action, delegation or response from observed results. - Decision owner: The Aeven session model actor, with first-party spawned/forked child actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cordis plugin composition, queued turn/step driver, tool execution, durable session logs, filesystem/shell/browser/MCP and host permission gates. - Closure path: Session log → request assembly → model tool calls → execution results → next step; child messages/reporting return to the parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The native loop owns iterative execution. Disabled Codex/Claude subagent rows and provider-internal control are not borrowed. - Evidence: packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml; packages/core/agent-loop/src/agent.ts; packages/core/agent-loop/src/tool-calls.ts; packages/subagent/tool-subagent/src/index.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: The released aeven preset mounts normal tools, native spawn/fork delegation and the first-party session loop; model/provider configuration completes ordinary deployment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent writable interference between parallel work actors in the supported installed swarm/best-of-N mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent workers or competing attempts would otherwise share writable artifacts and contaminate each other before selection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose nonoverlapping slices/separate output locations, keep attempts independent and decide subsequent retries/integration from returned evidence. - Decision owner: The parent model following the installed shipped skill; native subagent actors execute the separated briefs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: avn-swarm and avn-best-of-n packaged instructions, runtime subagents/workflow tools, filesystem/worktree commands and evidence-bearing reports. - Closure path: Parent allocates nonoverlapping briefs and private write locations → children act in their assigned locations → evidence/commit identity is checked → wrong/no-evidence results are rejected and respawned once or retained as gaps, then parent integrates accepted work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is explicit conflict attenuation with an agent-owned allocation/return policy, not S2 from generic messaging. No Agent Teams experimental service is needed. - Evidence: packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-swarm/SKILL.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-best-of-n/SKILL.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/README.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/catalog/index.json; packages/host/account-integrations/src/index.ts; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional account-installed catalog skills, not an always-active default guarantee; constraints are agent instructions, not filesystem sandbox proof.. - Boundary reachability: The official release skill catalog contains both ZIP bundles; Settings installCatalogSkill imports the bundled skill into account skills, which the supported session can load. Local bundle bytes were checked equal to authored instructions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current descendant commitments and intervene on behalf of the root task organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Running/idle/ready descendants, corrupt or unavailable records, delayed work and tasks needing redirection or interruption. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which current descendant work to interrupt and which direct child to steer or continue after inspecting current state. - Decision owner: The ancestor/root model actor within lineage-bound runtime authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listagents descendants projection, live/durable status, sendmessage, interruptagent, completion notification and authoritative lineage checks. - Closure path: Current tree/status/report → parent selects intervention/continuation → subagent service routes steering at a step boundary or requests cancellation → changed child activity and later reports return to the root. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The narrow credited function is current commitment regulation over the root task tree. It is not S3 from spawning alone, registry existence or a deterministic cancellation API without the model owner. - Evidence: packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/src/list-agents.ts; packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/src/index.ts; packages/subagent/subagent/src/control.ts; packages/subagent/subagent/src/continuation.ts; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Interrupt is asynchronous, stops only the target current turn and does not stop its children or drain queued messages. No human parent modifier is inferred from browser steering.. - Boundary reachability: The default aeven preset mounts list/control alongside continuable spawn/fork children. These tools use the live calling agent and recorded lineage; no custom supervisor or experimental Team layer is needed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complement author claims with an independent raw-diff review and a returned correction/re-review loop in the installed review skill mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-reported completion can hide bugs, wrong callers or goal failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The fresh reviewer challenges the actual code/diff; the author accepts or rebuts findings with primary evidence, fixes accepted findings and returns the changed artifact for review. - Decision owner: Separate fresh reviewer model plus author model with explicitly separated review/edit rights; a human resolves only an unresolved terminal dispute. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: avn-review-loop packaged brief, fixed base/diff, raw code/tests, per-finding ledger, four-round bound and no-edit reviewer rule. - Closure path: Author result → fresh-context read-only reviewer inspects raw changed code → APPROVE/CHANGES with file:line claims → author fixes/rebuts and reruns checks → reviewer rechecks open IDs/changed code; failures/timeouts are not approval. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The independently framed critic has materially different access to the artifact rather than repeating author prose; both judgment and returned correction are packaged instructions using supplied model actors. Deterministic CI and routine Ralph reports are not the credited auditor. - Evidence: packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-review-loop/SKILL.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/catalog/index.json; packages/skill/skill-catalog/README.md; packages/host/account-integrations/src/index.ts; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional installed skill mode, not automatic S3 for every session. Actual run compliance or independent approval is not asserted by this source assessment.. - Boundary reachability: The release catalog ships avn-review-loop.zip, verified byte-identical to its authored SKILL.md. Supported Settings/account installation and skill loading make the workflow reachable without custom review code; it can use the default fresh spawn provider.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective harness adaptation function is supplied at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The environmental/authority variety potentially addressed by the inspected paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right established in the assessed supported distribution. - Decision owner: No qualifying agent or parent owner established for this function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Background procedure distillation, user correction/slowness feedback, memory recall, capability candidates, lessons-to-checks and disabled self-extension tools. - Closure path: No corresponding function-specific organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The shipped learning path derives reusable procedures from completed tasks and corrections; it does not itself investigate prospective environmental alternatives or arbitrate them with present capability. Tool/web research remains S1 task execution unless a distinct adaptation case/return is established. Source-only developer self-extension and experimental packages do not donate an S4 mode. - Evidence: packages/capability/tool-capability/src/learner.ts; packages/capability/tool-capability/src/recall.ts; packages/capability/tool-capability/src/index.ts; packages/capability/tool-capability/README.md; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml; packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-lessons/SKILL.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity or ultimate-policy arbitration loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The environmental/authority variety potentially addressed by the inspected paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right established in the assessed supported distribution. - Decision owner: No qualifying agent or parent owner established for this function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persona/preferences, permission approvals, plan/goal requests, plugin/preset configuration and optional skill installation. - Closure path: No corresponding function-specific organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These define or configure the current assistant and authorize ordinary tasks/extensions; no runtime identity/ultimate-policy dispute is routed to a legitimate final authority and returned as a function-specific governing decision. Maintainer governance of Aeven releases is adjacent, not an operational S5 owner. - Evidence: packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml; docs/architecture.md; packages/core/agent-loop/src/agent.ts; packages/experimental/README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/06fccd0f6b1a06204de177306ddf2f00e96abdf0/assessments/ae/aeven.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 06fccd0f6b1a06204de177306ddf2f00e96abdf0\">06fccd0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T21:37:50+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/steepy-it/steepy-apex\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Steepy Apex</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/steepy-it/steepy-apex/tree/7283b7940e6ec97ec739a28b6de1525d2891bdb4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7283b7940e6ec97ec739a28b6de1525d2891bdb4\">7283b79 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: directly implement and test bounded target-project changes, or a representative first application path, using code-aware model discretion under explicit task/goal context. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository APIs, failing tests, incomplete specs, integration edge cases, platform setup and differing source conventions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a model specialist selects the actual code/test edits, investigates source ambiguity, runs red→green→refactor, and may choose a changed fix from reviewer feedback; a Gear 4 model worker selects a mutation attempt inside controller-owned constraints. - Decision owner: host-backed task-specific &lt;surface&gt;-agent in Gear 3; headless Claude/Codex/OpenCode coding agent in supported autopilot/Gear 4; research/bootstrap model coordinator or bounded children during inception. Not the JavaScript event controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: routed standards, task briefs, host model-tier mapping, adapters/headless.mjs, diff snapshots, work manifests, deterministic verifier and path/commit guards, task reports. - Closure path: accepted plan task and real source → model specialist implements with TDD/tests → writes report/diff → scoped reviewer returns a correction or approval → changed source verified; Gear 4 repeats a model mutation only when the mechanical goal check/review/budget permits. - Boundary reachability: README.md installs these first-party skills into supported coding hosts; skills/implement/SKILL.md dispatches the prompt-bearing specialist; scripts/autopilot.mjs and scripts/loop-engineer.mjs invoke headless host models on their documented supported subset. The emitted code edits and repository tests occur in the actual user&#39;s project. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves explicit task headers, guards and test commands but no source-specific code design or repair decisions; deterministic keep/discard and audit receipt parsing are not credited as autonomous implementation. - Evidence: implementer model prompt, implement task dispatch/recovery, headless runner, Gear 4 implementation loop, inception bootstrap. - Basis: first-party role prompt plus installed host invocation and executable implementation/feedback routes. - Confidence: high for shipped model ownership contract; medium for cross-host live execution without provider credentials. - Caveats: simulated/fake-runner tests demonstrate state handling, not third-party provider correctness. On Pi/DeepSeek, Gear 3/4 headless runners are explicitly unavailable; host tool absence reduces a delegate to same-session model work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: detect and correct incompatibility across distinct S1 implementation tasks contributing to one branch. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task A changes an interface or owned shared surface and Task B depends on its old API, silently undoes the change, or violates a cross-cutting standard; separate task-local TDD may still pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a fresh model whole-branch reviewer evaluates aggregate task integration and reports specific ISSUESFOUND; the model implement coordinator chooses an owning task fix and re-dispatches a specialist, then orders a new cross-task review. - Decision owner: separate model reviewer for cross-task mismatch judgment and model implement coordinator for corrective work selection; local status parsers and serial dispatch only enforce/report. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: one-implementer-in-flight discipline, task-result index, success-criteria-only artifact, aggregate Git branch diff, cross-surface standards, final-review-issues.md and fix manifests. - Closure path: completed independent task results → aggregate diff/criteria/standards reviewed for mutually contradictory interfaces → actionable finding artifact → affected specialist model fixes the shared boundary → new aggregate diff, fresh independent review and final verification. - Boundary reachability: installed Gear 3 implement calls final-review-prompt.md, regenerates the branch diff and loops issues into a task-local fix; the documented autopilot phase uses validated task/final reviewer manifests and reviewer-result guards. This is not inferred from a generic task queue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: explicit source-specific cross-task contract judgment and corrective task selection are model decisions, although task order and manifest/receipt constraints are deterministic. - Distinct S1 units: at least two separately bounded implementation tasks/possibly different &lt;surface&gt;-agent specialists in the same accepted plan, each owning an independent code/test deliverable (serial execution does not erase cumulative inter-task interference). - Inter-S1 disturbance: a later specialist&#39;s change to a shared interface or standards contradicts an earlier specialist&#39;s output, or its locally passing tests leave an aggregate integration fault. - Attenuating coordination relation: a model fresh-eyes whole-branch reviewer explicitly checks cross-task interface composition and contradictions against one aggregate diff, then supplies owning-task corrections. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: final-review-issues.md becomes a bounded fix manifest for the selected task&#39;s specialist; task output is revised, re-diffed and reviewed again. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the dedicated cross-task reviewer has the instruction to detect actual overlapping/interface incompatibility, and its issue artifact actively changes a task&#39;s implementation; mere one-at-a-time queueing or a status ledger does not supply the autonomous S2 witness. - Evidence: distinct task implementer role, whole-branch cross-task review, implement whole-branch fix closure, reviewer response evidence guards. - Basis: explicit cross-task interference/repair path, supported by source-level role separation and deterministic evidence validation. - Confidence: medium-high for live shipped model-mode relation. - Caveats: Gear 2 bypasses these fresh reviewers; same-session fallback on hosts without subagents weakens separation, and Gear 4&#39;s deterministic verifier alone should not be separately scored as S2.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: discretionary whole-current plan supervision, task/model-resource assignment, exceptional blocker escalation and whole-deliverable acceptance before handoff. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task complexity spread, unplanned discovery, one blocked specialist, incomplete planned work, review-fix churn, conflicting standards and over-spending strong model calls on mechanical changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main model implement coordinator reads the accepted plan, chooses task-specific specialist/model tier within routing limits, responds to NEEDSCONTEXT/BLOCKED by targeted context, tier escalation or splitting, assigns final cross-task fixes and controls whether the whole planned branch is ready for the next review phase. - Decision owner: the model executing installed Gear 3 implement skill; human has ratified spec/plan and owns protected scope/publication. Gear 3 JavaScript autopilot does not steer models; Gear 4&#39;s deterministic attempt budget, verifier and commit/stop do not themselves furnish autonomous S3 discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: accepted plan/task dependency order, task/result ledger, per-surface model-tier mapping, reporter and reviewer output contracts, task-results.mjs, autopilot-context.mjs, reviewer-response.mjs, host role dispatch. - Closure path: all current plan tasks/reviewer statuses and resource needs → main model prioritizes local task/specialist and escalates review findings or blockers → bounded work/fix dispatch changes current commitments and subsequently updated ledger → whole-branch verified review and exact review-phase handoff. - Boundary reachability: ordinary installed Gear 3 implement has explicit supervisory instructions and runs in a model session; headless Gear 3 autopilot starts the same model phase as a real child with its routed manifest. This is not an attempt to credit the deterministic Gear 4 controller as a model supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model decides actual current corrective assignments and risk-appropriate tiers under the whole plan, rather than a fixed plan→implement→review scheduler selecting those choices. Its human-approved scope remains binding; neither mere delegate invocation nor fixed budget logic is the evidence. - Whole-system current view: the coordinator sees the accepted multi-task plan, completed/pending ledger entries, task concerns, critical reviewer outcomes and whole-branch criteria/diff/approval status. - Current-control decision scope: sequencing and reassigning corrective work among specialists, model tier/escalation, splitting genuinely blocked tasks, and withholding next-phase completion until all task/branch gates are accepted. - Evidence: Gear 3 implement manager and escalation, model-tier/project flow, autopilot phase boundary, Gear 4 deterministic-rights separation. - Basis: explicit model-coordinator instructions with operational child/review return contracts, not the name controller. - Confidence: medium-high; no authenticated multi-model run executed during this assessment. - Caveats: this is Gear 3&#39;s model-owned current-control mode, not a claim that every host has full delegation; Gear 4 is deliberately a fixed deterministic loop after human goal/mutation authorization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: an independent source/diff/test evidence path that can reject an implementer&#39;s apparent completion and force correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic implementer reports, hidden missing tests, divergence between task claims and actual changed code, aggregate integration regression and verifier gaming. - Decisive decision or feedback right: fresh read-only task and final reviewers use exact diff, applicable standards and criteria to decide APPROVED versus ISSUESFOUND; Gear 4&#39;s separately dispatched model branch reviewer may identify goal gaming that a deterministic verifier missed. - Decision owner: fresh model reviewer distinct from code-writing implementer/loop worker on supported task-tool/headless modes; deterministic receipt digests and test commands bind evidence, but do not decide the semantic verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: captured Git diffs, independent code read access, criteria-only context, reviewer reports/issue artifacts, reviewer-response strict schema/baseline checks, original terminal verifier output, mandatory validate-hub.mjs in later review. - Closure path: reported task completion → separate reviewer directly inspects code diff/source and checks plan requirements → approval or actionable issue file → main model controller re-dispatches fix and re-audits; final phase independently re-runs test/hub commands before PR offer. - Boundary reachability: skills/implement dispatches task-reviewer-prompt.md and final-review-prompt.md to fresh task tools where supported, and the headless Gear 4 controller dispatches a separate injected reviewer. These are installed, active role paths, not test-only fixtures. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer model owns substantive cross-check findings from a separate direct evidence path; the same-session fallback and deterministic validators are weaker and are not counted as the independent model case. - Claim being audited: one task/whole branch is correctly implemented, tested and compliant with approved criteria, or a Gear 4 apparent verifier success is genuine. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer&#39;s DONE/TDD report and work ledger plus a mechanical verifier&#39;s pass/fail. - Complementary access path: fresh reviewer reads task/aggregate Git diff and relevant repository source plus standards, with separate criteria-only manifest; Gear 4 supplies immutable branch snapshot and model review evidence not reducible to the verifier exit code. - Independence boundary: reviewer prompt is read-only and separate from code writer with a new context; it has independently derived diff/criteria inputs, not unlimited authority, and may use the same vendor/model family. If host has no task-tool, the documented inline pass is weaker and does not prove this mode. - Who acts on findings: implement model coordinator reads issue file, assigns task fixes and reruns review; deterministic Gear 4 controller can grant only budgeted follow-up mutation; later review skill withholds publication. - Evidence: task reviewer, whole-branch reviewer, Gear 4 independent reviewer, controller snapshot/feedback, final evidence gate. - Basis: direct-evidence separately prompted review with corrections fed back to operating work. - Confidence: high structural, medium live reviewer independence without authenticated host dispatch. - Caveats: a routine deterministic validate-hub test alone is not S3, and successful fake-runner/CLI tests are not evidence that every provider delivers a fresh independent model.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: in the first-party inception mode, research external technology/version support and compatibility options for a prospective target-project architecture, obtain legitimate parent selection, and return that choice as the current approved implementation baseline. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing runtime/framework/host support, unsupported/deprecated package versions, mismatched toolchains, future deploy constraints and incompatibility between a planned app and feasible external technology. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user/project owner confirms externally researched architecture alternatives and version combination; the model researcher gathers official-source evidence and options but does not own the foundational adaptation choice. - Decision owner: the legitimate human project parent in the dedicated inception research/architecture/approval mode. No agent-autonomous S4 alternative is established in this release. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skills/inception/research-prompt.md official-source checks, version/date/support comparison table, decision register DR-n, helper-frozen approval/verification contract, component/coverage records, later certified hub import; model-mapping verifier detects host drift but does not automatically change shipped mappings. - Closure path: official ecosystem version/support/compatibility facts + future application/deploy requirements → model-supported option/research report → project human selects/approves foundation/version table → inception-state.mjs seals the approved project/contract → subsequent bootstrap uses selected versions and can be re-approved on substantial technology change. - Boundary reachability: inception is an installed skill invoked explicitly before init, with research step, user version-table confirmation, an approval gate and a real bootstrap/verification return. It is not Steepy maintainer roadmap or an example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research may be model-run, but the consequential capability/stack adaptation choice is explicitly reserved for a parent; the model cannot silently pick unsupported versions or change approved foundation. - External distinction: current official releases, model/harness/framework support life cycles, compatibility constraints and upstream project/tool documentation are checked as external facts, not inferred from the local code tree. - Future / prospective distinction: chosen stack longevity, forthcoming deploy requirements and support/EOL risk are assessed as compatibility for the application&#39;s prospective operation, rather than only a current failing task. - Adaptation option generated: the researcher and model coordinator prepare version/framework alternatives with explicit support status, latest stable version, compatibility experiments and reasons for deviation; the parent selects a foundation. - Path back into current capability / S3: the parent-confirmed versions and architectural selection become a sealed project/verification contract, govern real bootstrap commands and current implementation, and require reapproval when a foundational choice must change. - Evidence: inception research/options and approval, model researcher from official external sources, whole lifecycle/parent version choice, state/approval enforcement, mapping drift explicit patch policy. - Basis: parent-governed prospective capability-selection/return loop, bounded to inception and major-stack reapproval. - Confidence: medium; this is targeted externally verified first-project capability formation, not an ongoing autonomous environmental intelligence agency. - Caveats: ordinary Gear 3 code discovery/brainstorm and routine model mapping drift alerts are not autonomous S4; follow-on hub work does not automatically re-run inception or adapt Steepy itself.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: ratify and preserve the target project&#39;s ultimate purpose, architectural boundaries, security/identity/access design and acceptable change policy, with parent arbitration and return into subsequent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changed project scope or purpose, newly proposed auth/data/deployment boundary, conflict with existing hub standards, an unapproved fundamental technology/commit-policy change or a reduced verification requirement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the legitimate user approves complete project documents and contract, must re-approve substantial design/scope/identity/commit-policy shifts, and approves each promoted inception decision or later explicit doc-wins override; the model cannot self-approve. - Decision owner: project owner/operator as parent authority, not the worker, JavaScript status checker or autonomous implementation coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen full-document approvals, stable DR-n decision register, inception-state.mjs strict guards, verification-contract checks, certified init governance, discovery per-item accepted write-back, persistent .apex/standards/conventions.md plus CONFLICT stop rule. - Closure path: identity-level architecture/purpose/permission change is surfaced in the inception decision register or as a hub conflict → legitimate parent selects/approves exact documents (or corrected hub preview) → helper freezes identity and gates bootstrap/change → approved decisions are certified/promoted to durable hub standards and subsequent model code work must obey them. - Boundary reachability: shipped inception user dialogue and inception-state approve are invoked on a real target project; init and discovery explicitly verify-and-promote the same approved decisions into the ordinary installed workflow&#39;s constraints. Parent S5 exists even when later Gear 4 coding is headless. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model is the proposer/coordinator and may recommend options, but ultimate project identity and altered governing rules require explicit human affirmation; this is an identity-level documentary/operational return, unlike a one-off PR permission prompt. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: system purpose and shape, identity/access and security policy, deploy/design boundaries, and changed foundational commitments require a different authorized project charter; this is not mere task approval. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent-governed inception is the only claimed positive S5 mode; the actual target-project human owner authoritatively approves complete architecture/policy documents and subsequent foundational revisions, and approves their hub promotion. - Return-to-operation path: helper-frozen project and verification contract constrain all bootstrap and future material changes; certified init and discovery promote approved decisions to .apex/ standards that govern later coverage/CONFLICT classification and code implementation. - Evidence: inception human approval/reapproval, inception approval commands and scope, certified policy return, verified governance promotion, doc-wins policy in normal workflow. - Basis: explicit, parent-governed identity-level choice plus recorded cross-mode return to future agent work. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped protocol; provenance of a human statement remains an operational trust assumption, as the helper explicitly notes. - Caveats: commit authorization, ordinary spec acceptance, PR offer, verifier pass/fail, and branch-only blast radius alone would not qualify as S5. The credited S5 path is deeper inception identity/reapproval → certified governed hub; it is not Steepy&#39;s OSS license/maintainer policy.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/44e7bcb8bf11ae8486406a23ba9998751555f51f/assessments/st/steepy-apex.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 44e7bcb8bf11ae8486406a23ba9998751555f51f\">44e7bcb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T17:58:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/revfactory/codex-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness for Codex</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/revfactory/codex-harness/tree/80f8217b5b6d3f220a31f05f9c0adaf09dac40f4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 80f8217b5b6d3f220a31f05f9c0adaf09dac40f4\">80f8217 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Explore source, design bounded implementation, write assigned code/tests or conduct narrowly scoped technical verification as actual project work units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown source architecture, technical errors, implementation alternatives, failing project tests, individual task acceptance details and changed inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The project&#39;s model-backed explorer/architect/worker/QA agents choose source inspection, code modifications and test actions within a refreshed task packet and role permissions, returning findings, files and checks. - Decision owner: Native Codex subagent instantiated with a first-party harness role and assigned project-local packet; parent coordinates, and generic Codex model/tool runtime supplies inference/execution transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: .codex/agents TOML, plugin/skill invocation, work scopes, workspace-write or read-only role sandbox requests, native spawn/follow-up, run/input/result records, actual agent IDs and test commands. - Closure path: User goal → $harness target-project setup and model parent task breakdown → role-specific packet to native child → model-selected project read/write/check work → evidence/actual output to parent → accepted result or returned correction. - Boundary reachability: Plugin marketplace and skill explicitly enable target-project bootstrap; docs/multi-agent.md and skills/harness/SKILL.md describe actual native spawn and current-project prerequisites, including first-spawn preflight and operational fallback. Seed roles are valid TOMLs installed into the project, not merely benchmark sample agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The Python run ledger can check contracts and fingerprints but cannot select code edits or semantic analysis. The first-party agent role plus model execution is the operational owner; an unstarted agent definition alone would not be. - Evidence: installed plugin and $harness invocation; harness Phase 2/3 and first child preflight; bounded worker role; read-only explorer and QA roles. - Basis: Explicit installed model-backed native worker path and differentiated job rights; dynamic task actions returned to parent. - Confidence: High for shipped wiring, medium for live behavior across user-specific Codex clients and permission settings. - Caveats: Actual native role discovery, spawn capability, parent cwd and runtime limits must pass; source includes guarded fallback that must not be reported as a live multi-agent PASS. This assessment itself did not execute any model task.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Suppress actual/structurally evidenced collisions between two current task workers&#39; shared-file ownership, interface expectations or write succession, and return a revised conflict-free contract to affected S1s. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent worker A and B both trying to update a shared config/type/lockfile, incompatible interface assumptions, one worker&#39;s dependency output changing while another has begun, or competing edits to a shared workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The first-party parent is directed to assign one owner to shared files, serialize overlapping work, inspect a combined diff when concurrent contributions conflict, negotiate affected interface contracts and reassign/return updates to the appropriate workers. Peers report collisions and propose changes but do not silently overwrite. - Decision owner: Model-backed parent using explicit collaboration/ownership rules when mediating a concrete inter-worker conflict; deterministic dependency ledger and messaging helper support but do not decide semantic repair. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Disjoint ownership file sets, shared-interface agreements, actual native peer messages, parent-contract confirmation, ready/dependency checks, worktree preservation instruction, change fingerprint invalidation and recorded ownership transfers. - Closure path: Worker A&#39;s discovered shared-interface/file conflict with Worker B → direct peer event/parent report → parent examines source and shared contract, selects owner/serial order or revised interface → new current packets/explicit native follow-ups alter A/B actions → integrated source/test evidence closes the collision. - Boundary reachability: docs/multi-agent.md explicitly mandates single ownership for shared types/manifests/lockfiles and says out-of-scope edits go to the parent for reassignment/serialization. Its failure handling separately instructs the parent to inspect combined diffs after actual concurrent edits collide. harnessworker specifically reports a concurrent ownership overlap, and canonical skill Phase 5 makes parent contract confirmation/worker handoff part of supported runtime behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The S2 claim is not credit for a mailbox, task DAG or parent delegation. The parent makes a discretionary dispute-specific ownership/interface reconciliation based on different workers&#39; potentially incompatible changes; scripts merely record ownership and deliver refreshed packets. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more native harnessworker agents assigned separate project modules/parts of a dependency wave; independently execute and return their artifacts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Both need the same shared interface/config/file, or one worker&#39;s new contract invalidates another&#39;s assumptions; concurrent edits would corrupt or contradict the integrated source. - Attenuating coordination relation: Single shared-file owner plus negotiated interface and serial handoff, with parent examination/reassignment if conflict is observed, not indiscriminate shared-write access. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Parent-confirmed revised ownership/dependencies and newly refreshed task packets dictate which worker may change the contested file and when dependent agents may proceed. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The intervention addresses a named inter-worker interference — overlapping writes/shared interface contradiction — and changes both workers&#39; subsequent allowed work; generic ready-queue execution or communication logging would not settle the contested source contract. - Evidence: shared types and out-of-ownership return; collision handling and parent reconciliation; worker explicit ownership-overlap return; skill Phase 2 and Phase 5 parent contract confirmation. - Basis: Differentiated first-party parent-owned inter-S1 collision mitigation with explicit returned file/contract changes. - Confidence: Medium; behavior is a real installed model-role contract, not independently measured actual conflict-resolution performance. - Caveats: run.py metadata validation alone does not choose a conflict resolution. File scopes constrain behavior by instructions, not OS-enforced per-path ACLs; agent mistakes remain possible and user policies may force manual repair.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Govern this project&#39;s current run across worker cells: priority/ready choices, commitment/ownership, available subagent capacity, integration, correction rounds and terminal acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Global task dependency drift, incomplete or stale outputs, multiple workers contending for current resources, model/tool incompatibilities, uneven progress, review/QA failures and inability to satisfy acceptance criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The parent Codex session reads the whole current goal, plan, dependencies, input fingerprints, accepted decisions, actual native agent states and returned evidence; it selects tasks to dispatch within capacity, verifies predecessor completion, returns specific defects to workers or performs permitted fallback, and accepts/blocks final integration. - Decision owner: First-party harness-configured parent model session, distinct from the deterministic run.py ready/result/resume checks and from an individual implementation worker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run plan and task packets, actual native spawn IDs, 3-thread requested capacity with real runtime recheck, agents registry, dependencies and fingerprints, explicit message correlation, bounded retry limits and completion validator. - Closure path: Parent receives current state and worker/reviewer reports → chooses ready assignments, current interface/commitment correction, retry or escalation → native follow-up or agent launch directs S1 work → updated outputs/status, original tests and reviewer findings return → parent re-evaluates integration/closure. - Boundary reachability: Canonical skill Phase 5 makes the parent the active executor of task dispatch, current capacity control and final work acceptance after explicit invocation; repository .codex/config.toml and AGENTS pointer route the parent to the skill. Both plugin and installer modes have a documented path to actual Codex role/tool use. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Merely storing a plan, rate-limiting workers or printing a ready queue would not choose semantic task priorities, reconcile present interface changes or decide review-driven worker rework. Those choices are assigned to the executing model parent. - Whole-system current view: The one project run&#39;s objective, input fingerprints, dependency DAG, contracts, all registered live worker states, results, shared-file ownership, review and QA evidence, known blockers and remaining budget. - Current-control decision scope: Which task/role runs now, reallocation after failure/capacity, accepted interface changes, dependency readiness, updated work packets, bounded verification/rework and final current-run outcome; not product-market strategy or enterprise identity. - Evidence: parent role and actual scheduling/handoffs; Phase 5 integration and decision path; run registry and input freshness rules; configuration and target-project activation. - Basis: Active parent model with current-run-wide resource/commitment and rework decisions; deterministic state gives auditability, not autonomy on its own. - Confidence: Medium-high for the first-party mandate, moderate for live runtime compatibility in arbitrary Codex deployments. - Caveats: Parent must be in target-project cwd with actual role discovery/spawn tools; the source warns that absent native tooling leads to a limited fallback and no fabricated multi-agent success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Challenge an implementer&#39;s source/correctness/completeness claims through a separate read-only reviewer and return causal findings into parent-controlled correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect or overstated worker success claims, missed regressions, mismatched interfaces, unsafe permissions assumptions, implementation artifacts that fail acceptance despite an apparently completed task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separately instantiated harnessreviewer has a read-only context/role and directly examines assigned diff, surrounding code and acceptance criteria; it issues severity/file/line/trigger/impact/remedy findings to the parent. QA may independently run boundary tests and return actual check evidence. - Decision owner: The first-party reviewer role executed as its own native Codex subagent; parent uses returned findings to choose corrective assignment, while validate.py only checks structural/evidence completeness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harnessreviewer.toml sandbox request read-only, direct diff/code inspection contract, refreshed review packet, no implementation rights, independent child context where supported, distinct QA role, returned findings and parent recheck loop. - Closure path: Worker edits project and reports success → parent commissions distinct read-only reviewer after integration → reviewer independently inspects real source/diff and acceptance boundaries → findings returned directly to parent → parent sends defects back to owning worker → changes and re-review/test outcomes update terminal acceptance. - Boundary reachability: Shipped reviewer TOML and canonical skill Phase 5/6 specify independent review after modules/integration. docs/multi-agent.md explicitly invokes reviewer/QA and routes defects to owning workers; plugin installation with actual native role use can instantiate the reviewer without developing a new capability. This is a normal bounded mode, not an external optional code-review vendor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer makes new semantic judgments from original code evidence, rather than validating the implementer&#39;s own response or passing a static schema. The read-only role cannot silently rewrite its target change during review. - Claim being audited: Worker/parent&#39;s assertion that the changed code and current integration satisfy specified behavior and acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker result status, summary, changed files, own test report and current-run ledger. - Complementary access path: Distinct reviewer reads actual source/diff and criteria directly; QA can run observable tests independently, with findings not restricted to the worker&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: Dedicated harnessreviewer native role/context and requested read-only sandbox; shared Codex provider/models are possible, effective runtime policy may differ and the parent chooses reviewer task scope. - Who acts on findings: Parent owns acceptance and routes confirmed defects back to the implementation owner; worker implements corrections, then parent rechecks relevant boundary tests/audit. - Evidence: reviewer original diff and independent findings contract; QA role limits; integrate/review/route corrections; Phase 6 verification and rework. - Basis: First-party, separate reviewer with complementary raw artifact access and returned implementation correction loop. - Confidence: High for specified audit role/feedback, not measured reviewer effectiveness. - Caveats: A reviewer with read-only role may need a separately configured runtime to achieve effective isolation; no proof of model/vendor independence or live successful audit was generated by this Index assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would autonomously scan the changing future environment, develop strategic capability options and return them to this task organization&#39;s current operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future model/tool ecosystem, user markets and external technology shifts beyond the declared project-local current work objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Phase 0/1 inspects the target project&#39;s current source, Phase 2 chooses reusable local agent architecture, and Phase 7 handles user-requested drift/maintenance. No distinct future/outside intelligence owner with a standing prospective return loop is established at the current-run recursion. - Decision owner: Parent project operator instructs harness creation/updating and sets enduring purpose; first-party parent selects current local role/tool design to execute the requested task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Domain audit, role/skill creation, six architecture templates, explicit subsequent modification/drift review, retained project-local role instructions and validations. - Closure path: Current project inspection leads to reusable roles and skills used for the requested project tasks; later user-triggered audit can revise them. This is project-local capability provisioning, not an autonomous externally prospective strategy-to-current S3 return loop. - Boundary reachability: The explicit factory capability is real and operating, but targets a known current project and user request; generic potential use for future work does not show future external sensing and option selection. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-backed local harness design can be agent-owned S1 engineering work and may affect later executions, but that alone lacks the future environmental distinction, adaptation-option choice and S3 conversation required for S4. - Evidence: Phase 0/1 source audit and domain analysis; Phase 7 on-demand evolution and drift; actual bounded invocation contract. - Basis: Negative for external/future strategic intelligence; legitimate agent-authored present task&#39;s reusable infrastructure distinguished. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: At a differently defined long-term harness-maintenance organization, Phase 7 plus an explicit future environment sensing agent would merit a separate S4 review; no such owner was proven here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would adjudicate the ultimate identity and legitimate governing purpose of the project organization, with binding return to current work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Deep disputes about mission, legitimate parent authority, principal values or constitutional constraints beyond ordinary code/task permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User request establishes the project/harness purpose, and inherited Codex tool/sandbox permissions determine allowable operations; the parent enforces current task contracts and can escalate, but does not originate ultimate policy. - Decision owner: Human project owner/operator and applicable higher-level Codex/user policy remain outside the first-party current work-run organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role sandbox requests, skill boundaries, user approval/consent, Codex parent session, project-local AGENTS.md, target-workdir safety, forbidden privilege escalation and tool availability checks. - Closure path: Parent follows existing user authorization and role permissions and may stop/escalate work; no evidence of an identity dispute deliberated and returned as an enduring policy decision across subsequent projects. - Boundary reachability: First-party permission and target-project checks are reachable and explicit, but they enforce already accepted instructions rather than empowering a separate legitimate-policy actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model role&#39;s current acceptance decisions and framework instruction texts are not authority to change organizational purpose; inherited permissions and human approvals are constraints, not a first-party S5 choice. - Evidence: user explicit invocation and project scope; parent responsibilities and platform permissions; AGENTS project guidance. - Basis: Negative for ultimate identity-policy authority at one project-local run recursion. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Parent model&#39;s final delivery answer and a user&#39;s approval are current task/parent inputs, not automatically published S5=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f73dbb3d1779142b6c572cde0b6f330855e731b8/assessments/ha/harness-for-codex.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f73dbb3d1779142b6c572cde0b6f330855e731b8\">f73dbb3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T16:39:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dragonked2/alphacode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AlphaCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dragonked2/alphacode/tree/a28db8978a5b336e244973c8db01f625dfcc0983\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a28db8978a5b336e244973c8db01f625dfcc0983\">a28db89 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform software-engineering and related browser/desktop automation through model-selected tools and actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, implementation choices, command/test failures, browser/desktop state, provider/model variation, task ambiguity and bounded worker assignments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select substantive coding/tool actions, interpret operational evidence and determine how to satisfy the assigned engineering objective. - Decision owner: the active model-backed AlphaCode coding agent; spawned swarm workers own their assigned operational workstreams. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in tool registry, provider routing, sessions, permissions/safety gates, context management, goal contracts, budgets and runtime cancellation. - Closure path: user/coordinator goal → model chooses coding/tool actions → first-party runtime executes → evidence returns → model revises implementation or reports completion. - Boundary reachability: ordinary AlphaCode sessions and spawned first-party swarm workers instantiate the same shipped coding runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes and constrains actions; the model owns the open-ended engineering decision. - Evidence: README; agent.rs; tool registry. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external MCP/browser/provider services remain dependencies and are not credited as S1 owners.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrent swarm S1 units through model-selected graph decomposition, dependencies and assignment/takeover choices backed by first-party assignment and dependency enforcement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate active assignment, two workers concurrently editing overlapping work, dependency-order violations, overloaded workers, stale/dead assignees and integration/synthesis ordering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task graph decomposition and dependency edges, select or request worker assignment, and explicitly reassign/replace/retry work when current peer interaction requires adjustment. - Decision owner: the model-backed swarm coordinator through the model-callable swarm tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan graph readiness, assignment-load ranking, in-flight target claims, active-assignment conflict rejection, worker heartbeats/staleness, dependency blocking/unblocking and typed node artifacts. - Closure path: coordinator defines graph/assignments → runtime observes peer assignment/dependency/liveness state → conflicting or non-runnable work is blocked/rejected/reclaimed → coordinator receives current graph/worker feedback and can reassign, retry, replace, spawn or restructure → affected S1 work proceeds under the revised relation. - Boundary reachability: the shipped swarm tool exposes taskgraph, assigntask, assignnext, runplan and task-control actions directly to the model; default swarm workers are first-party inline/headless AlphaCode sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code enforces conflict and readiness rules, but the coordinator model chooses the organizational partition, dependencies and substantive takeover/recovery action. - Distinct S1 units: the coordinator/main coding agent and concurrently spawned first-party swarm worker coding agents, each owning a bounded task-graph node or workstream. - Inter-S1 disturbance: active double assignment can make two workers edit the same work concurrently; dependencies, worker load/liveness and overlapping responsibility can also cause unsafe or stranded work. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-selected task/dependency boundaries plus exclusive active assignment, load-aware worker selection, explicit takeover/reassignment and dependency gating. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected double assignment prevents a second worker from starting; completion/failure/staleness changes graph readiness; reassign/replace tells the displaced worker to stand down and directs subsequent execution to another worker. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path explicitly regulates concrete peer-worker interference and dependency/liveness conflicts rather than merely moving messages or decomposing a parent task. - Evidence: communicate.rs; commcontrol.rs; commgraph.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: low-level target claims and conflict rejection are deterministic support; the A classification rests on model ownership of graph/assignment/recovery choices.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current multi-worker organization by maintaining a whole-swarm view and intervening in active commitments, worker allocation, retries, replacement and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed, stale, crashed or overloaded workers; blocked graph nodes; incomplete artifacts; low-confidence work; current concurrency pressure; stranded assignments and changing ready frontiers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect whole-plan/member/context state and choose current assignment, spawn, stop, retry, reassign, replace, salvage, graph expansion or other current commitment action. - Decision owner: the model-backed swarm coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: planstatus/taskgraph summaries, member status, heartbeats, artifact metadata, readcontext/summary, runplan progress, task-control server transitions, concurrency budgets and resource guards. - Closure path: coordinator observes current plan plus worker status/artifacts → chooses current-control action → first-party server mutates assignment/worker/graph state and interrupts or dispatches workers as required → subsequent current execution reflects the decision. - Boundary reachability: the coordinator model receives the shipped swarm tool containing status, planstatus, summary/readcontext, runplan, spawn/stop and task-control actions; graph seeding can elect the seeding model session coordinator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: schedulers and resource guards enforce state transitions, but the model coordinator can inspect the whole current organization and choose the substantive commitment/recovery intervention. - Whole-system current view: swarm plan status exposes node readiness/status/dependencies/assignees plus low-confidence debt, while member status, heartbeats, summaries and readcontext expose current worker state and evidence. - Current-control decision scope: worker/task assignment and replacement, retries/salvage, spawning/stopping agents, concurrency use, graph expansion and current integration/synthesis commitments. - Evidence: communicate.rs; commcontrol.rs; commplan.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic runplan loops and resource monitors also make current decisions, but the positive A claim does not depend on treating those enforcement components as organizational owners.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge completed deep-swarm work through separate critique/verify workers and force additional operational work when gaps remain. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete coverage, unsupported worker claims, low-confidence artifacts, missed edge cases and gaps hidden by ordinary worker completion reports. - Decisive decision or feedback right: probe the audited scope and either inject concrete follow-up nodes or issue a passing gate artifact accounting for every audited node. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed deep-mode critique/verify gate worker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auto-inserted gate nodes, typed artifacts, audited-id accounting, low-confidence debt checks, completenode validation and injectgap graph mutation. - Closure path: production nodes complete with artifacts → independent gate worker receives an explicit audit scope and probes it → gaps cause injectgap and new operational nodes → after those drain the gate re-runs; only a sufficiently accounted clean artifact closes the gate → parent/composite work can then synthesize or finish. - Boundary reachability: deep taskgraph is a shipped swarm mode; deep expansion auto-inserts critique/verify gates that are dispatched through the same first-party worker infrastructure with gate-specific instructions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic server rules reject thin passes, but the separate gate model decides what to probe, whether a substantive gap exists and which follow-up nodes are needed. - Claim being audited: that the completed sibling/node artifacts sufficiently cover their assigned scope and are ready for synthesis/closure. - Ordinary reporting path: each production worker returns its typed completion artifact, validation, confidence and whatididnotcheck list. - Complementary access path: a separately dispatched full AlphaCode gate worker receives the aggregate audit scope, can use its own coding/repository tools to probe claims, and is instructed to seek gaps rather than accept the ordinary reports. - Independence boundary: the gate is a separate worker/model session with a distinct adversarial role and explicit audited scope; it is not the author worker checking its own completion. - Who acts on findings: the gate worker itself can inject new task-graph nodes; the coordinator/runtime dispatches those nodes and prevents the parent scope from closing until they drain. - Evidence: swarm core; commcontrol.rs; commgraph.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the ordinary deterministic report claim checker is not used for this state; S3 rests on the separate adversarial deep-gate worker with direct first-party operational tooling.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed swarm/coding recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying future/external distinction is shown being turned by a distinct adaptation owner into options that return to change current organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent memory, web/browser tools, ambient scheduling, selfimprove/selfdev tools, provider/tool discovery and project-analysis helpers may influence later work but are not sufficient S4 closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-improvement and memory can change or preserve behavior, but the Profile explicitly requires an external-and-prospective option-development conversation with present capability; the inspected paths remain task/current-repository, persistence or scheduled-execution mechanisms. - Evidence: memory tool; selfimprove tool; ambient manager. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: web/ambient/selfdev surfaces are powerful extension and automation mechanisms, but no standard future-oriented adaptation loop into S3 was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established at the coding/swarm recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy conflict is shown reaching an authoritative owner and returning as governing runtime policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: safety gates, destructive-action checks, permissions, system prompts, configuration, goals and resource budgets constrain ordinary operation but do not create identity authority. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents can adapt tactics and organize workers but cannot authoritatively redefine AlphaCode&#39;s identity or ultimate operating principles. - Evidence: README safety section; tool registry; resource monitor. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user configuration/approval and safety policy are legitimate constraints but do not establish S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6dac75ca3fbfaf4412ec9666b6681415c7477b9a/assessments/alphacode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6dac75ca3fbfaf4412ec9666b6681415c7477b9a\">6dac75c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T22:29:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hizliemre/horse-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Horse Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hizliemre/horse-code/tree/8e134ac3b2745477940bda5867313d07ae535900\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8e134ac3b2745477940bda5867313d07ae535900\">8e134ac &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement software-development tasks through model-selected repository/tool actions until reviewed work is ready to merge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, implementation choices, repository constraints, build/test failures, review feedback, tool/model failures and merge-local changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete code changes and tool actions, interpret returned repository/test evidence, revise the implementation and decide when a task attempt is ready for review. - Decision owner: the active Horse Code implementer role agent (for example coder, designer, senior-coder or principal-coder depending on the stage). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role registries, tool permissions, isolated worktrees, board state, model fallbacks, turn budgets, automatic checkpoints, code-review/acceptance gates and git merge machinery. - Closure path: task + project context → implementer chooses tool/code actions → worktree produces concrete results → review/test feedback returns → implementer/revision actor changes work → accepted work merges into the session base. - Boundary reachability: standard feature/bugfix jobs construct role agents and task worktrees through the shipped job, wave-task, task-cycle and agent-loop paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic worktree, board and git machinery transports and constrains the attempt, but removing the role model removes the discretionary coding choices that create the implementation. - Evidence: README.md; src/engine/task-cycle.ts; src/engine/wave-task.ts; src/agent/loop.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: role/model routing and runtime guards can change which agent serves an attempt, but they do not replace the implementer&#39;s open-ended operational decision right.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among parallel implementer S1 units before and during concurrent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two operational task agents may be scheduled together despite an unstated semantic dependency or may attempt to write overlapping files, causing stale assumptions, failed downstream tasks or merge conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether candidate parallel tasks have a missing dependency that makes simultaneous execution unsafe and add the dependency edge that changes their subsequent scheduling. - Decision owner: the model-backed team-lead role for semantic dependency coordination. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project-manager file/dependency declarations, deterministic computeWaves, splitFileConflicts, live busy-file exclusion, dependency-ready scheduling, isolated worktrees and serialized merge sections. - Closure path: board exposes candidate parallel tasks/dependencies/write paths → team-lead audits the interaction → accepted missing dependency is added to the board → wave computation/scheduler observes the new edge → affected implementer S1s no longer run in the conflicting relation. - Boundary reachability: team-lead audit is invoked by the standard board/wave pipeline before parallel implementation and writes directly to the first-party Board consumed by execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the scheduler and file exclusion deterministically enforce already-known relations; the semantic decision that a task actually needs another task&#39;s output is produced by the team-lead agent. - Evidence: src/engine/project-manager.ts; src/engine/team-lead.ts; src/engine/waves.ts; src/engine/wave-engine.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: same-file exclusion is deterministic support and is not used by itself to claim agent ownership. - Distinct S1 units: parallel coder/designer/other implementer agents executing different board cards in separate task worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: missing semantic dependencies and overlapping write sets can make concurrently executing cards interfere, consume stale state or collide at merge. - Attenuating coordination relation: team-lead adds missing task dependencies; deterministic wave/file-clash machinery then serializes the affected relationship. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the changed Board dependencies and busy-file relation alter eligibility, so an affected implementer starts later against merged predecessor state instead of continuing in the unsafe parallel relation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path is tied to a concrete interaction disturbance between distinct parallel operational units and changes their interaction topology; generic board storage and role routing are not the positive witness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate current whole-job resource pressure by changing how many operational task agents may run concurrently. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a job can expose more runnable cards than the available machine/model/provider capacity can safely support, producing heap pressure, rate limits and a larger unfinished tail. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the live maximum number of task agents allowed in flight for the current/future scheduling passes of the running job. - Decision owner: the human operator in the supported interactive TUI mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TUI board/running-agent display, /parallel N, the mutable parallelRef, persisted maxParallel, wave-engine slot accounting and the scheduler&#39;s live ceiling() getter. - Closure path: current run exposes active agents/board state → operator chooses a new concurrency ceiling → setParallel updates the live value → the scheduler rereads that ceiling on subsequent passes → current task admission/resource usage changes. - Boundary reachability: /parallel is a shipped TUI command explicitly documented to take effect on the running job. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic scheduling enforces the ceiling, but the discretionary whole-run resource choice belongs to the operator. No first-party autonomous actor was established that owns an equivalent whole-system current resource/commitment judgment. - Evidence: README.md; src/tui/commands.ts; src/tui/app.tsx; src/engine/wave-engine.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: team-lead dependency repair is credited to S2, while deterministic park/wake/retry, model-health and slot machinery support present operations without owning S3 discretion. - Whole-system current view: the TUI surfaces the persistent board and currently running agents/models for the job while the operator can issue run-control commands. - Current-control decision scope: the operator changes the global in-flight task ceiling that governs resource allocation across the job, with immediate effect on later scheduling passes.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the integrated result after local task review and return whole-diff findings into corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: locally reviewed task outputs can each look acceptable while their merged combination still contains cross-task defects, deferred review concerns or integrated-surface problems. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the merged base diff holistically and decide approve versus request-changes; after bounded revision rounds, decide whether remaining findings are acceptable or require a human decision. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed principal-coder reviewer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only reviewer tools, merged/base diff construction, persisted revision card, PR review comments/threads, senior-coder revision loop, commit/push and repeat review. - Closure path: task-level work merges → principal-coder receives the integrated diff plus deferred findings → autonomous audit verdict/comments → senior-coder applies changes → changes are committed/refreshed → principal re-reviews or makes a bounded final audit decision. - Boundary reachability: the standard job path invokes the PR revision pass whenever merged work exists, even on partial jobs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the principal-coder is a distinct read-only judgment role from the operational implementers and from the senior-coder that applies corrections; git/thread machinery only records and transports its findings. - Evidence: src/engine/job.ts; src/engine/revision.ts; src/engine/reviewer.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary per-task code review and acceptance gates are not independently sufficient for S3. The positive witness is the additional integrated post-merge audit over the combined result. - Claim being audited: that the merged job result, not merely each individual task, is coherent and ready to ship. - Ordinary reporting path: task implementers plus per-task code review/acceptance report individual card outcomes and merge them into the base. - Complementary access path: the principal-coder later reads the combined base/PR diff holistically, including deferred local findings. - Independence boundary: principal-coder is a separate read-only reviewer from the task implementers and the senior-coder revision actor, and it observes the integrated artifact after local review/merge. - Who acts on findings: senior-coder applies requested corrections; the principal-coder then re-audits the changed integrated result, with unresolved ownership decisions eventually reaching the user.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: adapt Horse Code&#39;s future role capability to changing external model availability/capability and project-specific skill needs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: connected model sources change availability, quota, cost/capability and source diversity; projects differ in whether skills such as testing or UI guidance are appropriate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose model chains for all roles from the currently healthy external model pool and choose which installed skills should become durable role capabilities for the scanned project. - Decision owner: the model-backed tuner used by /roles adjust (with a separate model-backed skill tuner for skill assignment). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-source discovery, model-health quarantine/probing, capability/source heuristics, project factual scan, assignment validation, role registries and config persistence. - Closure path: current external model pool + project facts → tuner reasons over role requirements and adaptation choices → validated model/skill assignments are applied to live role registries → saved configuration changes future sessions and later S1/S2/S3 operation. - Boundary reachability: /roles adjust is a shipped TUI path; it calls the first-party tuners, applies their returned chains/skills and persists them. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic validators constrain invalid or unsafe assignments, but the role-to-model and role-to-skill selection is produced by model reasoning rather than fixed configuration or the operator choosing each assignment. - Evidence: src/tui/app.tsx; src/engine/role-tuner.ts; src/engine/skill-tuner.ts; src/engine/project-scan.ts; src/engine/model-health.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the operator explicitly invokes /roles adjust; that trigger is not the decisive adaptation judgment. Manual /roles setmodel and config edits are not separately promoted to a parent S4 mode here. - External distinction: which connected/provider-backed models are currently healthy/routable and what installed capability/skill inventory is available. - Future / prospective distinction: which role-capability allocation will better survive later workload, rate-limit/source failures and project-specific task demands. - Adaptation option generated: a validated three-model chain per role plus project-appropriate skill assignments. - Path back into current capability / S3: assignments are applied to live role registries and persisted, altering the agents/resources available to later operational, coordination, review and control activity.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish or amend the project&#39;s standing constitution so later Horse Code roles are governed by the returned project-level principles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a project may need new or revised standing principles, coding/governance rules or identity-level constraints that ordinary task execution should not decide locally. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine the requested project-governance/constitutional direction and any core-principle choices that the analyst cannot legitimately infer. - Decision owner: the human user/operator as legitimate parent project authority in the supported govern path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: intent classification to govern, analyst constitution phase, askuser, committed constitution artifact, constitution parsing/scoping/caching and rule injection into role prompts. - Closure path: user raises a governance/constitution matter → analyst carries that request and asks the parent when core principle decisions are needed → constitution is established/minimally amended → subsequent role prompts receive the applicable rules → later operation is governed by the returned project policy. - Boundary reachability: the govern intent and constitution phase are shipped first-party lanes, and ordinary feature runs also load the same constitution into governed role prompts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the analyst authors and operationalizes the document, but the legitimate ultimate authority for project principles remains the user; automatic initial constitution authoring is not treated as evidence that the analyst has acquired ultimate project identity authority. - Evidence: src/engine/upstream.ts; src/speckit/phases.ts; src/engine/constitution.ts; src/engine/constitution-store.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a static constitution file alone would not establish S5. The positive path is the supported parent governance lane plus return of the authoritative result into later runtime prompts. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: establishment or amendment of the project&#39;s own standing principles and governing rules rather than an ordinary implementation choice. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the human project user/operator is the ultimate authority in the claimed parent-governed mode; no separate autonomous S5 base mode is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: the resulting constitution is stored in the project, scoped into applicable rules and injected into later agent prompts so subsequent work is governed by that decision.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f0d20ee58a50fe8c6c03f2bcaf8fa1091c7d8fd0/assessments/horse-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f0d20ee58a50fe8c6c03f2bcaf8fa1091c7d8fd0\">f0d20ee &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-30T13:28:42Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ApodexAI/FrontierAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FrontierAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ApodexAI/FrontierAgent/tree/9e533db6f6c34d16037ee5ec964c479d0eb51cde\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9e533db6f6c34d16037ee5ec964c479d0eb51cde\">9e533db &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: independently perform bounded open-ended research, analysis, coding/file and evidence-gathering assignments using model reasoning and tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task instructions, web/file/tool observations, failures, intermediate evidence, sandbox state, assigned output constraints and follow-up questions from the coordinator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific search, tool, file, computation or report action and revise later actions from returned observations within the delegated assignment. - Decision owner: each model-backed Agent Team sub-agent session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SwarmSubagentRuntime; first-party agent loop; per-session specialist prompts; allowed-tool sets; private worktrees; task/session budgets; submitreport; context compaction/recovery; AgentBus dispatch. - Closure path: coordinator assignment + original question → sub-agent model chooses task-specific actions → first-party tool/runtime executes them → observations return to that sub-agent session → the model revises its approach until it submits a report or reaches a runtime stop condition. - Boundary reachability: Agent Team is a documented native workflow in README.md and ships ready profiles whose coordinator can create/assign sub-agents with first-party research/file tools; no user-authored orchestration code is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the sub-agent model while retaining AgentBus, sandboxing, queues and tool executors removes the open-ended choice of what evidence/action to pursue next. Deterministic runtime components constrain the work but do not own its task-specific decisions. - Evidence: README.md; subagentruntime.py; assigntask.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic recovery/finalization fallbacks can terminate a failed sub-agent, but ordinary task ownership remains model-backed and feedback-driven.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate concrete interference among distinct S1 work units, including contradictory evidence and competing authority to publish the same final deliverable. - Distinct S1 units: independently running model-backed Agent Team sub-agent sessions assigned separate research/file tasks, including parallel investigators, a selected publisher, and specialized verifier sessions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: distinct sub-agents can return contradictory claims/derivations, and independently active workers could otherwise contend for the same final /outputs deliverable or retain stale publication authority after reassignment. - Attenuating coordination relation: the coordinator routes disagreements to a dedicated localverifier with both reports attached and assigns follow-up work from that arbitration; for output contention it grants exactly one structured outputpaths manifest while runtime publication state/locking excludes competing publishers and forces all others workspace-only. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: verifier findings change which claim the coordinator accepts and what follow-up assignments it dispatches; publication arbitration changes which sub-agent can write /outputs, which agents remain workspace-only, and whether publisher work is reused, delayed, or transferred. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited paths regulate identified interference among otherwise autonomous S1 outcomes/commitments and close the resolution back into those units&#39; later work or write authority; mere fan-out, messaging and the task board are not used by themselves as S2 evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incompatible sub-agent reports/derivations; overlapping or contradictory evidence; concurrent attempts to own/write the run&#39;s final output manifest; stale publisher ownership during reassignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the coordinator decides when a disagreement requires separate arbitration and which S1 receives follow-up work/publication authority; a dedicated localverifier independently resolves the contested claim, while runtime publication guards enforce the coordinator&#39;s single-publisher allocation. - Decision owner: the model-backed Agent Team coordinator for coordination strategy and assignment; the separately model-backed localverifier for the disputed-evidence arbitration it is assigned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: teameffort: max conflict policy; &lt;attach agent=&quot;...&quot;/&gt; report transfer; LOCALVERIFIER specialist prompt; task-board owner changes; structured outputpaths grant; publication lock/state; one-publisher guard; workspace-only routing for non-publishers; verifier publish prohibition. - Closure path: distinct S1 reports conflict → coordinator detects the disagreement and spawns/assigns localverifier with both reports → verifier re-derives/source-checks and returns an arbitration report → coordinator updates the task/evidence plan and later assignments from that result. For file contention, coordinator grants one exact output manifest → runtime blocks overlapping publisher ownership → affected agents execute either publisher or workspace-only roles accordingly. - Boundary reachability: all three shipped Agent Team profiles set teameffort: max, which injects the conflict-verifier strategy into the coordinator contract; assigntask and its publication guards are first-party tools in those shipped profiles. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic locks alone would only serialize access, but here the model-backed coordinator decides which evidence conflict to arbitrate, whether to dispatch more work and which agent owns the publisher commitment. Removing that agent-owned decision while retaining the lock/task board removes the adaptive coordination policy. - Evidence: prompts.py; assigntask.py; taskboard.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every parallel assignment is S2. The positive finding is limited to the concrete conflict/arbitration and publication-interference loops above.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a current whole-run view of work and regulate present commitments, staffing and priorities across the Agent Team. - Whole-system current view: the coordinator receives the run-wide task board with open/in-progress/resolved/cancelled commitments and owners, live AgentBus session/job status, collected/failed/running reports, publication ownership, and remaining runtime/budget signals across the Agent Team. - Current-control decision scope: the coordinator can add/cancel/reopen tasks, replace owners, create/reuse/stop sub-agents, dispatch another evidence wave, wait or proceed, and select/transfer the final publisher, thereby changing current resource allocation and operational commitments across the run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfinished or newly emergent tasks, weak evidence, stalled/off-track agents, exhausted sessions, failed assignments, conflicting reports, publication responsibility and shrinking runtime/time budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which work remains open/resolved/cancelled, add new work, choose/replace owners, dispatch or reuse agents, stop an active agent, launch another evidence wave, wait for reports, and select/transfer the final publisher subject to first-party safety guards. - Decision owner: the model-backed Agent Team coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live task board with resolutions/owners and agent/job status; createsubagent; assigntask; collectreports; stopsubagent; addtask/updatetask; AgentBus; SpawnGuard; session/task caps; wall/budget observers; publication-state guard. - Closure path: task board + live agent/job/report state → coordinator compares current coverage/commitments with the user objective → coordinator changes owners/status, dispatches follow-up/new agents, stops/reuses/reassigns work or changes publisher → AgentBus/task-board/publication state applies those choices → subsequent S1 work proceeds under the revised commitments and returns new reports/state. - Boundary reachability: the documented native Agent Team workflow exposes these tools to the coordinator in shipped simple, tui and benchmark profiles; the task board is always on even though shipped profiles disable the optional two-phase planning mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AgentBus, task limits and the board mechanically represent/enforce state, but the coordinator chooses which current disturbance matters and how to reallocate commitments. Removing the coordinator model leaves resource caps and queues but removes adaptive whole-run prioritization/reassignment. - Evidence: README.md; workflows/agentteam/README.md; taskboard.py; stopsubagent.py; assigntask.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the S3 finding is not based on delegation or result aggregation alone; it is based on the coordinator&#39;s current whole-run state plus actual commitment/reassignment/stop/publication rights.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the integrated answer/evidence before completion and return corrective findings to current control. - Claim being audited: the coordinator&#39;s integrated draft and its load-bearing factual, computational and sourced claims before the Agent Team presents the final answer/deliverable. - Ordinary reporting path: research/worker sub-agents return assignment reports to the coordinator, which synthesizes those reports into the current draft and manages the ordinary task board/commitment loop. - Complementary access path: a separately instantiated finalverifier receives the original question plus the complete draft, then uses its own verifier prompt and independent/different queries, source checks or re-derivations rather than merely accepting the workers&#39; reports. - Independence boundary: verifier role names are routed to a dedicated first-party verifier system prompt, verifiers are prohibited from publishing the deliverable, and the audit runs in a separate model-backed session from the coordinator and ordinary workers; the optional planning gate can additionally require verifier participation. - Who acts on findings: the model-backed coordinator consumes verifier findings, repairs the synthesis or dispatches new S1 research on failed points, updates present commitments, and can send the corrected result through another verification pass. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported or incorrect final claims, arithmetic/factual errors, weak sourcing, contradictions surviving synthesis and other defects in the coordinator&#39;s draft. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the separately instantiated finalverifier decides whether claims survive independent re-derivation/source checking and reports concrete failures; the coordinator must then repair/research the failed part and can re-verify before finalizing. - Decision owner: the model-backed finalverifier sub-agent for audit findings; the coordinator owns the corrective response to those findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reserved verifier role names; automatic specialist prompt routing; FINALVERIFIER role contract; independent/different-query verification guidance; verifier publication prohibition; report handoff; coordinator repair/re-verification loop; optional planning-mode finalizegate that additionally hard-blocks completion without verifier participation. - Closure path: coordinator forms a complete draft → creates/assigns finalverifier with the original question and draft → verifier independently checks load-bearing claims from sources/re-derivation and returns a report → coordinator repairs substantive gaps by revising synthesis or dispatching another S1 wave → corrected answer can be re-verified before completion. - Boundary reachability: all shipped Agent Team profiles set teameffort: max, whose standard coordinator contract explicitly calls for a final verifier and repair/re-verification. Optional planning mode strengthens this with a deterministic finalize gate, but that non-default gate is supporting evidence rather than the sole basis of the positive state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier is a separate model-backed session with a dedicated verifier prompt and no publication authority; it does not merely echo the coordinator&#39;s normal synthesis path. Removing the verifier model leaves logging/guards but removes the independent challenge and finding generation. - Evidence: prompts.py; createsubagent.py; assigntask.py; finalizeanswer.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: shipped profiles keep planningmode: false, so the verifier requirement is model-contract enforced there rather than deterministically blocked by finalizegate. The verifier itself is nevertheless a first-party, separately prompted, boundary-reachable audit actor with corrective return.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop established at the declared Agent Team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate adaptation signals include changing external information, repeated task outcomes, benchmark results, runtime failures, reusable session history, skills and operator steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor was found that owns external/future modelling and converts it into a persistent change to FrontierAgent&#39;s operational strategy, tool/skill repertoire or organizational configuration, then returns that adaptation through S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web research; reusable sub-agent sessions; context/history/checkpoints; skills loading; benchmark/evaluation surfaces; user steering; profile configuration. - Closure path: not established. Agents can research external facts for the current task and retain/reuse current-run context, while skills/profiles are loaded from configured artifacts; no standard first-party loop was found that autonomously generates/selects and persists a future-oriented organizational adaptation for later operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task research and evidence-driven follow-up are S1/S3 activity, not S4 by themselves. Memory/checkpoints and operator-supplied skills preserve or configure capability but do not create an agent-owned outside-and-then adaptation conversation. - Evidence: README.md; workflows/agentteam/README.md; frontieragent/components/skills/; subagentruntime.py. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the runtime has useful ingredients for future adaptive machinery, but ingredients and generic external access are not credited as S4 without the complete prospective adaptation closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy closure established for one Agent Team run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate policy matters include tool permissions, approval requirements, execution/sandbox policy, role naming, model/profile configuration and publication authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party agent or parent path was found with authority to resolve identity/purpose/ultimate-policy questions for the runtime and return the result as authoritative policy governing subsequent operation. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: operator-authored profiles; execution/tool permissions; approval gates; sandbox constraints; role/session identities; publication manifests; runtime limits. - Closure path: operational policy/configuration constrains tools and writes, but it is supplied/enforced as configuration. No identity-level issue is escalated to legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a policy decision by the assessed runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: technical agent/session identity, system prompts, permissions and approval enforcement are operational boundaries. They do not by themselves constitute S5 identity and ultimate-policy ownership. - Evidence: README.md; workflows/agentteam/profiles/; assigntask.py. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a runtime-recursion finding; it does not claim the OSS project or its maintainers lack human governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f7ed4df5f68a71f3d2fd4c93f371058c05ef8593/assessments/frontieragent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f7ed4df5f68a71f3d2fd4c93f371058c05ef8593\">f7ed4df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T20:10:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Loqira-Labs/agentkot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KOT</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Loqira-Labs/agentkot/tree/7cf18c4b8389a81ea2f15ab70d1b0bb31b44b6ab\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7cf18c4b8389a81ea2f15ab70d1b0bb31b44b6ab\">7cf18c4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform user objectives into code/file/process/research outcomes through repeated model-selected tool actions and returned operational evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, changing workspace/process state, tool errors/results, provider/model outputs, prior session evidence, context pressure and follow-up input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool/action, arguments and stopping point, revising later actions from returned tool/environment evidence. - Decision owner: the active KOT session model; child models own the same local operational discretion within their assigned work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shipped executable, built-in tool schemas/executors, persistent sessions, History/Memory/Plan, background-process management, provider adapters and Web UI. - Closure path: user/session context → model selects tool/action → KOT executes and records the result → later model step observes that evidence → model revises the next operation or concludes. - Boundary reachability: the executable and built-in model/tool surface are the standard shipped product, not benchmark/development-only machinery. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model removes task-specific selection of tools, arguments and completion; the runtime executes and persists those choices rather than replacing them with a fixed procedure. - Evidence: README.md; tools/Agent.md; built-in tool documentation. - Basis: explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrently executing child S1s by assigning dependencies/owners and, when operations may collide in a shared project, selecting isolated worktree execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ready workflow steps/delegates/teammates can operate concurrently; without isolation, inherited child cwd and disk-synced edits can overlap in the same project state and invalidate peer work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which child work may run concurrently and whether a child/step receives isolation:&quot;worktree&quot; / transactional worktree execution, plus define task dependencies that delay dependent work. - Decision owner: the orchestrating lead model when issuing Agent/Plan calls; for declared workflows, the first-party workflow declaration supplies the chosen dependency/isolation relation and KOT enforces it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate child sessions, git worktree creation/cleanup, workflow DAG dependency scheduler, shared Plan board, serialized task mutation and child result/mail feedback. - Closure path: lead identifies/decomposes multiple tasks → assigns dependencies and/or selects a dedicated worktree for an interfering child → KOT schedules ready work and enforces isolated cwd/branch where selected → child results/status return to the lead/shared board → subsequent work is integrated, redirected or delayed accordingly. - Boundary reachability: Agent{spawn|delegate|hire} and workflow transactional/worktree controls are documented built-in capabilities of the shipped executable; no downstream coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: KOT deterministically creates the requested worktree and respects declared DAG edges, but the task-specific judgment that work is independent or needs isolation/dependency belongs to the lead model/declaration. The runtime does not infer equivalent semantic isolation from the task text. - Evidence: tools/Agent.md; tools/Plan.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: worktree isolation is optional; credit is for the shipped model-selectable coordination relation, not for generic concurrency or messaging alone. - Distinct S1 units: the lead session plus multiple separately running child/delegate/teammate sessions, each driven by its own model and tool loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrently ready children may otherwise edit the same inherited project workspace or consume/alter shared operational state in incompatible ways. - Attenuating coordination relation: model/declaration-selected git-worktree isolation, plus explicit workflow/Plan dependencies and owners that constrain which work becomes ready together. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child terminal notifications, mail, shared task-board state and workflow state return coordinated outcomes; failed/dependent/isolation outcomes alter later lead/child work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation targets a concrete concurrent shared-workspace interference mode and changes where/when affected S1 units may operate; mail and child spawning alone are not credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current portfolio of owned child/delegate/teammate work, including commitments, lifecycle, task ownership and model/provider resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing child progress/liveness, pending mail, failed or obsolete commitments, blocked tasks, provider/model suitability, workflow pause/failure and need to redirect or terminate current work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create/withhold child commitments; inspect current owned-agent status; send new instructions; stop work; change a live teammate&#39;s model/provider; mutate task owners/dependencies; inspect/stop/resume workflows. - Decision owner: the lead/orchestrating model for its owned domain, with optional human ownership only where an explicit workflow ask/escalation step is declared. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent status/send/stop/setmodel, durable child registry/session ids, shared Plan board, workflow status/stop/resume, mail router and Web current-state panels. - Closure path: lead creates current commitments → status/task/workflow views return present state → lead chooses steering, reassignment, resource/model change, stop or further delegation → KOT applies it → subsequent child/workflow operation changes. - Boundary reachability: these operations are model-facing built-ins of the shipped Agent/Plan surface and are also exposed in standard Web controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: lifecycle services enforce stop/mail/model transitions, but the substantive current-control choice over which commitment to redirect, terminate or re-resource is made by the lead model. - Evidence: tools/Agent.md; tools/Plan.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: KOT intentionally prevents arbitrary child re-orchestration; S3 is centered at the main lead session&#39;s declared ownership domain. - Whole-system current view: Agent{status} lists the lead&#39;s teammates and delegates with lifecycle/live state, model/provider, pending mail and wake errors; the Plan board and workflow status expose current task ownership/dependencies/run state for the same organization. - Current-control decision scope: child/team creation, current instruction steering, interruption/removal, task ownership/dependency mutation, workflow stop/resume and live teammate model/provider allocation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a delegated worker&#39;s claim/readiness for completion against direct operational checks and, when configured, a separate judge verdict before the loop is accepted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker can report plausible completion while the actual workspace fails the declared command/test or while a separate judge identifies a material reason to continue/escalate despite a passing direct check. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after direct working-directory verification, independently choose accept, continue with corrective specifics, or escalate failure. - Decision owner: the configured supervisor agent; the built-in arbiter is explicitly the judge role and may use a distinct model/provider. The direct command check is deterministic evidence/support rather than the autonomous judgment owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate until loop, direct command execution in worker cwd after every turn, no-progress detection, strict verdict parser and worker feedback/retry loop. - Closure path: worker turn → KOT independently executes declared check in worker cwd → failed check output returns to worker, or passing check reaches supervisor → supervisor verdict continue returns specifics to next worker turn, escalate fails, accept permits completion. - Boundary reachability: until and supervisor are standard documented parameters of the shipped delegate operation; arbiter is a built-in agent type, so no downstream reviewer implementation is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic exit-code evaluation supplies complementary operational evidence, while the optional separate supervisor owns the discretionary post-check audit verdict; removing that supervisor removes the independent accept/continue/escalate judgment while leaving the check machinery intact. - Evidence: tools/Agent.md. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: S3 is established in the supported delegate+until+supervisor mode; ordinary delegates need not exercise it. - Claim being audited: that the delegated worker&#39;s assigned result satisfies the declared completion condition and is ready to be accepted. - Ordinary reporting path: worker turns and the delegate&#39;s eventual task notification/report. - Complementary access path: KOT executes the declared command directly in the worker&#39;s working directory after each turn; a separate supervisor then receives the passing-gate situation and issues a strict verdict. - Independence boundary: the check observes workspace behavior outside the worker&#39;s self-report, and the supervisor is a separately launched agent/judge that can use a different model/provider and has its own tools/context. - Who acts on findings: KOT feeds failed-check or continue findings into the worker&#39;s next turn; escalate fails the loop; only accept closes completion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established in the shipped KOT boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external information, prior sessions, persistent notes and available provider/model catalogs can inform current work, but no shipped mechanism closes a future-oriented change to KOT&#39;s organizational capability or governing structure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: none established at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Search, History, Memory, model/provider discovery, user model overrides and durable workflows expand/reconfigure current task capability but do not by themselves own adaptation. - Closure path: no qualifying external/prospective option → adaptation decision → present capability change → subsequent operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistent memory and retrieval carry knowledge forward, but they do not establish a distinct external intelligence function with authority to redesign current organizational capability. - Evidence: README.md; tools/Memory.md; tools/History.md; tools/Agent.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity / ultimate-policy closure is established for the KOT organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/provider/settings, user notes, workflow ask gates and host operating rights constrain individual operation but do not constitute an identity/ultimate-policy issue resolved by legitimate ultimate authority and returned into later operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: none established at the assessed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configuration files, provider/model selection, persistent user-scope Memory notes, workflow ask/escalation and ordinary user control of the host process. - Closure path: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy issue → ultimate authority → authoritative policy decision → returned operational governance loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead model can select operational resources and record memory but does not own the ultimate right to redefine KOT&#39;s organizational identity or governing policy. - Evidence: README.md; tools/Memory.md; tools/Agent.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9b6a02efd6a9023ceb7cfe3bf9dedeb380aecb82/assessments/kot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9b6a02efd6a9023ceb7cfe3bf9dedeb380aecb82\">9b6a02e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:54:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mana-byte/theater\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Theater</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mana-byte/theater/tree/405d8b914e588e91befba6f8a82eb56ff563fd6e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 405d8b914e588e91befba6f8a82eb56ff563fd6e\">405d8b9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: connected model-backed CLI agents perform variable coding/research/testing tasks and return artifacts/results in Theater job sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code states, user specifications, tool outcomes, test failures and revised task briefs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each active worker chooses concrete coding actions, tools/tests and corrective edits within its authorized scope. - Decision owner: spawned or attached Claude/Codex/Pi/opencode/Vibe agent session, not Theater daemon transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verified terminal/provider route, MCP spawn/send/await operations, transcript tracking and optional worktree provisioning. - Closure path: agent gets task/brief, acts in its own harness, reports done or blocked, and receives further corrections via Theater send/follow-up. - Boundary reachability: installed Theater explicitly supports these five CLI harnesses and exposes agent-facing spawn/await tools; attached autonomous agents are a first-party operating mode, not an unspecified API consumer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: attached model/tool sessions own work decisions; Theater&#39;s deterministic process and identity service only makes them addressable and observable. - Evidence: README operating modes, delegation toolset, worker play card. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source review, not a live multi-provider run; an idle process/terminal alone is not an operational S1 unit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: the hierarchy master dynamically separates concurrent S1 coding streams by path ownership, live interface dependencies and shared integration constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel agents can edit overlapping files or consume interfaces changed by another stream; merge order and shared hot files create conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: master selects light/heavy execution, assigns exclusive owned paths, keeps shared hot files at integration, sequences consumers after producers, and rejects/repairs unauthorized diff overlap. - Decision owner: model-backed hierarchy master using the packaged opt-in hierarchy/master protocol; deterministic worktree/plugin gates support rather than replace it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stream-base SHAs, worktree isolation, dependency contracts, exact file ownership audit, Beads board and role-scoped agent messaging. - Closure path: after detecting dependency/overlap, master reallocates paths or sequences work, sends bounded tasks, audits completed stream diffs against ownership, then integrates reviewed SHAs in dependency order; failed conflicts return for repair. - Boundary reachability: Theater ships the hierarchy skill and Beads plugin as its own packaged theater-plus option; base daemon by itself is only C-level transport and no default autonomous S2 actor is claimed for unconfigured sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the optional installed hierarchy master makes situational shared-resource and inter-S1 dependency decisions; registry route checks and worktree naming are deterministic supports. - Distinct S1 units: independent workers/leads in separately launched model sessions/worktrees on a common integration project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: worker B consumes a function worker A changes, or two streams touch one shared registry/hot file. - Attenuating coordination relation: master-enforced path contracts, producer-before-consumer ordering, exclusive integration ownership and exact-SHA merge sequence. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: findings/failed path audits are sent to the owning worker for correction; dependent tasks wait for accepted producer commits. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the master explicitly evaluates cross-worker resource/interface collisions and changes parallel work assignment/merge topology to prevent them. - Evidence: hierarchy master track/build/integrate, play contract, Beads plugin. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: opt-in Beads/hierarchy deployment and model compliance required; base Theater without that play does not autonomously coordinate path ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: master agent supervises current task commitments, acceptance criteria, multi-worker results and final integration; the human operator can override task priorities/approval in a separate legitimate mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked workers, unreviewed commits, drifting acceptance criteria, failing merged suites and open user decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: master chooses work allocation, requests corrections, freezes exact reviewed heads and integrates approved streams; human Régie/brief decision can accept/refuse scope, publication or task continuation. - Decision owner: connected hierarchy master agent in the optional packaged play; parent human user via Régie/interactive instructions and explicit authorization for external merge/push. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: participant tree, await handles and structured results, role/position permissions, Beads progress board, integration worktree, human messaging/interruption. - Closure path: master polls/awaits the job handles, checks actual SHAs and tests, revises/reviews current work, merges exact accepted heads, reports AC closure to user; parent can send new scope/decisions or stop work, with authorization returned to master. - Boundary reachability: hierarchy master is a shipped first-party play in optional Theater-plus; Régie and MCP controls are shipped operational interfaces, not an inferred human board. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-backed master makes current-control choices while deterministic daemon enforces identity/routing; parent authority remains human for material scope, publication and shutdown outside given permissions. - Whole-system current view: live agent tree, job result handles, master-scoped shared task board, integration branch status, unit acceptance criteria and review outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: current task decomposition, simultaneous work budget, integration order, review gating, remediation and final closure. - Evidence: hierarchy master, Régie operator interface, MCP management controls. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: base daemon&#39;s tree/dashboard alone is not autonomous S3; optional master mode and legitimate owner intervention are distinct paths.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independent reviewer agent inspects builder&#39;s actual commit/diff/tests and returns located evidence-based findings for correction before acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a builder reports completion despite incorrect code, missing checks or unacceptable deviations from the brief. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer reads the raw git diff and reruns checks, issues approve/changesrequest tied to exact SHA and refuses agreement while substantiated blockers remain. - Decision owner: separate model-backed reviewer in shipped pair play or hierarchy review role, distinct from builder and denied write ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate role/model requirement, read-only reviewer worktree, exact-head artifact binding, structured review reports, corrective send and re-review. - Closure path: reviewer independently reads git diff base..HEAD, observes tests, sends located findings to builder, verifies revised SHA after correction and approves only exact unchanged head; master gates integration. - Boundary reachability: theater-delegate installs and invokes the built-in pair reviewer play directly; optional hierarchy also mandates independent per-stream and final reviewers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an independent active agent inspects source artifacts rather than trusting completion text; its judgments have an actionable return loop to the worker/master. - Claim being audited: builder&#39;s delivered implementation and compliance with acceptance criteria at named commit. - Ordinary reporting path: builder&#39;s done and structured result. - Complementary access path: reviewer reads raw source/diff and runs acceptance checks in the separate reviewer role; receipt/commit validation is independent of builder&#39;s report. - Independence boundary: reviewer cannot edit, uses a different model from builder under pair protocol, is not a dual-role writer; human approval does not substitute for audit. - Who acts on findings: same builder repairs or rebuts; reviewer re-checks, and coordinating master rejects unreviewed integration. - Evidence: pair reviewer, delegate launcher, hierarchy review. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: audit succeeds only with correct model/provider availability and role compliance; this review did not behaviorally execute a pair.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective environment sensing, future option generation and committed adaptation return loop is packaged. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent market, model capability and future task landscape may change, but supported controls are current task/model selection and activity observation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate intelligence actor is required to observe external change and choose future capability shifts for the organization. - Decision owner: human may change configs; master can conduct optional research when asked, neither is a standing installed S4 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model discovery, logs/trajectories, cost view and user-defined skills. - Closure path: provider/model choice affects future spawns but is not a packaged outside-environment-evidence -&gt; prospective options -&gt; S3-adaptation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: live observability and model lists show present status/options; future-oriented learning is generic capability the user must request and compose. - Evidence: architecture, README model/discovery controls. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a separately authored prospective skill could use Theater tools without becoming a function of the standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: role/position policies, identities and operator authorizations exist but no first-party whole-organization ultimate-policy deliberation/return loop is shown. - Disturbance / variety regulated: delegation rights, user consent, play membership and sensitive termination risk are controlled as task/session authorization rather than an institutional identity-policy tension. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party pathway takes a cross-system identity/ethos issue through legitimate ultimate authority and returns binding organization-level policy to work. - Decision owner: user can choose permission and play configuration; daemon enforces role constraints, not a separate S5 policy judge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: play membership and position assignment, retirement rights, job approvals, operator choices and provider trust boundaries. - Closure path: a specific human permission changes what one operation may do; no S5 issue -&gt; legitimate deciding forum -&gt; returned policy instrument is packaged. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: organizational hierarchy and named master do not prove ultimate policy authority; safe controls and membership constraints are lower-level operation governance. - Evidence: play position contract, hierarchy play, architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a specific externally configured organization could implement S5 over this substrate, not evidenced here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/060b1c404ce6e66e69d8aa369f42ad764d580265/assessments/th/theater.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 060b1c404ce6e66e69d8aa369f42ad764d580265\">060b1c4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T22:14:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/itissika/litecode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LiteCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/itissika/litecode/tree/146a9feff9edee2204138f18473b9bef83c41c5a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 146a9feff9edee2204138f18473b9bef83c41c5a\">146a9fe &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work on the selected workspace through open-ended model/tool action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous repository structure, incomplete requirements, implementation alternatives, file contents, command/build/test/LSP results, tool/permission failures, context limits and changing workspace state encountered while completing coding work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which project evidence to inspect, which offered tool/action to invoke, what implementation or command to attempt, how to respond to returned evidence and when the assigned operational outcome is complete. - Decision owner: the model-backed LiteCode primary agent for ordinary work and each separately instantiated model-backed child session for its delegated bounded outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent profiles/tool bindings, model adapters, context pipeline, permission engine, durable session store, snapshots/revert, LSP/search engines, tool pipeline, cancellation and step limits. - Closure path: task plus current workspace/session context → model selects a tool/action → LiteCode executes or rejects it → tool/environment result is persisted into the same session → the model receives the returned evidence and selects the next action or final response. - Boundary reachability: ordinary CLI/server/web/desktop turns instantiate the shipped first-party loop directly; child sessions use the same first-party session/turn primitives rather than an adopter-authored agent runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed actor while retaining tools, state and permission machinery removes the open-ended task-specific software-engineering judgment. - Evidence: README.md; src/agent/core.rs; src/runtime/mod.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider inference is external; the assessment credits LiteCode&#39;s first-party role/tool/feedback composition, not provider internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among concurrently executing child coding S1 cells by autonomously assigning non-overlapping write responsibility and reacting when a worker crosses or collides with that boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple mutation-capable child sessions can operate concurrently on one workspace and otherwise overwrite the same files/regions, act on stale overlapping assumptions or create incoherent parallel changes. - Distinct S1 units: separately instantiated general (and user-defined permitted) child sessions, each with its own durable session/context/turn and independent model/tool execution. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent mutation-capable children share the project workspace; overlapping writes or ownership scopes can make one worker invalidate another&#39;s work. - Attenuating coordination relation: the shipped orchestrator prompt requires the manager to choose team structure, who touches which files, who is read-only/writable and to maintain “one writer per file region at a time”; assignments carry explicit constraints/write scope to each child. Process-wide file locks additionally reject simultaneous structured writes/edits to the same file across sessions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the orchestrator&#39;s assignment changes the concrete file scope a child is instructed to touch; live corrections can be sent or the child stopped. If two sessions nevertheless collide through structured write/edit, the blocked tool result names the holder session and tells the affected child to retry after the other writer, so later S1 action changes under the coordination result. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping is tied to an explicit shared-file writer-interference problem and a first-party manager policy plus cross-session exclusion specifically aimed at that interference, not to the mere existence of child sessions, queues or messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the live team/write-scope partition—who may write which region and when a running worker must be corrected or stopped to preserve that partition. - Decision owner: the model-backed shipped orchestrator primary agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagentlaunch responsibility/prompt contract; orchestrator board/write-scope guidance; child role/tool ceilings; process-wide WorkspaceWriteLock; structured resource keys; completion/status/message/stop paths. - Closure path: orchestrator identifies parallel work and interference risk → assigns distinct roles/write scopes in child prompts/board → children act under those scopes → current status/results or a write-lock conflict returns → orchestrator sends correction/stops/reassigns as needed → subsequent child behavior follows the revised coordination. - Boundary reachability: orchestrator is a seeded first-party primary profile with general and explore in its allowed-subagent set and the full subagent tool series bound through the standard runtime; no consumer-authored coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the process lock is deterministic enforcement, but removing the orchestrator model removes the task-specific decision of how to partition real work/file responsibility and how to revise that relation as current team state changes. - Evidence: src/config/globaldb/builtinprompts.rs; src/config/globaldb/seed.rs; src/tools/subagent/launch.rs; src/tool/writelock.rs; src/tool/executor.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the hard file lock covers structured write/edit, not arbitrary shell-side mutation; S2=A therefore rests on the autonomous orchestrator&#39;s explicit write-scope/interference policy, with the lock as additional enforcement rather than sole evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current team commitments and interventions across the active child-session population on behalf of the user&#39;s present project goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: children may be running, idle, stopping, completed, failed, blocked, working on obsolete scope or returning evidence that changes which commitment should continue next. - Whole-system current view: subagentlist enumerates every descendant session visible to the primary with agent identity, responsibility, latest assignment preview, raw current session state, running turn age/step and terminal reason; child completions are also injected into the parent through the shared lifecycle source. - Current-control decision scope: choose team size/roles, launch work, inspect the current roster, continue an idle child with a new fitting assignment, send corrections, wait on a fixed result set, stop stuck/known-wrong work and synthesize/reallocate current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which current child commitments should exist, continue, be corrected, be stopped or be replaced as the user&#39;s active goal evolves. - Decision owner: the model-backed shipped orchestrator primary agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagentlist, subagentsend, subagentstop, subagentwait, durable child-session metadata/status, lifecycle completion router, todo/plan/board files and cancellation. - Closure path: current roster/completion state returns to orchestrator → orchestrator judges current allocation/intervention → launch/send/stop/wait/reassignment action changes active child commitments → later child operation/results reflect that decision and return to the manager. - Boundary reachability: the seeded orchestrator has the standard primary tool bindings and allowed general/explore children. Its shipped system prompt explicitly names team management, roster inspection, continuation and stopping as manager duties. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: session state, lifecycle routing and cancellation only expose/enforce facts. Removing the orchestrator model removes the semantic decision of which current commitments to create, continue, redirect or stop. - Evidence: src/config/globaldb/builtinprompts.rs; src/tools/subagent/list.rs; src/tools/subagent/send.rs; src/tools/subagent/stop.rs; src/tools/subagent/hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the human Workers roster is an observation/navigation surface and child panels are deliberately read-only, so no separate parent-governed S3 mode is credited from the UI.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation work through a fresh non-authoring child session assigned to verify specified claims/evidence, then return that verifier&#39;s result to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementation child can report completion while its own assumptions/tests miss defects, or while the manager lacks independent evidence that the delivered change satisfies done criteria. - Claim being audited: that another child/main implementation satisfies the specified coding requirement and verification criteria in the actual workspace. - Ordinary reporting path: the implementing child returns its own conclusion/tests/evidence through its normal durable child turn result. - Complementary access path: the shipped orchestrator policy explicitly requires verification to be “someone who did not write that code” and states that verification/unrelated work should use a new hire/new session; the orchestrator can launch a fresh general child with direct repository/test tools or a fresh read-only explore child for independent inspection. - Independence boundary: the verifier has a separate child session, fresh conversation/context and separately assigned responsibility rather than continuing the implementer&#39;s session. Provider family may be the same, so independence is contextual/access-path rather than model-provider diversity. - Who acts on findings: the orchestrator receives the verifier completion through the parent completion router/durable turn result and can accept, send corrective work, stop/replace a bad implementation path or launch another bounded fix before reporting completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make the semantic verification judgment from a fresh, non-authoring model context examining the actual project evidence requested by the manager. - Decision owner: the model-backed fresh verifier child selected/assigned by the autonomous orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shipped orchestrator verification rule; seeded general/explore roles; new child-session creation; responsibility/done-criteria prompts; direct read/search/bash/test tool access as role permits; completion routing. - Closure path: implementation result returns → orchestrator assigns explicit proof criteria to a fresh non-authoring child → verifier inspects/runs the relevant evidence and returns judgment → orchestrator uses that finding to accept or change current implementation work → subsequent operation changes accordingly. - Boundary reachability: the verifier is constructed from seeded first-party subagent roles via the ordinary subagentlaunch path, and the requirement for independent authorship is part of the shipped orchestrator prompt rather than consumer-authored coordination code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic session plumbing cannot decide whether arbitrary implementation evidence proves the requirement; removing the fresh verifier model removes the complementary semantic audit judgment. - Evidence: src/config/globaldb/builtinprompts.rs; src/config/globaldb/seed.rs; src/tools/subagent/launch.rs; src/tools/subagent/hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: verification is manager-chosen rather than a mandatory gate on every code change; the positive state relies on the explicit non-authoring/fresh-session rule and corrective return, not merely on generic subagent availability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then loop was established that senses external/future-relevant change, develops adaptation options and returns a selected option into LiteCode&#39;s present organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: session search, context compaction, web-capable explore workers, hot-plug provider/tool configuration, workspace engines and the persistent knowledge base expose information or preserve useful state, but none closes the required prospective organizational adaptation loop in the standard distribution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sessionsearch; websearch/webfetch on explore; project knowledge tool/files; workspace drift reminder; provider/model/agent settings; MCP/custom-tool definitions; code search/LSP readiness; context compaction. - Closure path: not applicable; no first-party external/future distinction → adaptation-option formation → selected persistent capability/strategy change → return into current S3/capability loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistent knowledge is explicitly described as human-owned; agent-created knowledge nodes begin pending and a person enables them. The drift detector tells the agent to notify/ask the user before organizing stale knowledge. Web exploration and session retrieval answer present work rather than autonomously adapting the organization for future environmental change. - Evidence: src/tools/knowledge.rs; src/knowledge/reminder.rs; src/config/globaldb/builtinprompts.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can compose research, knowledge and settings into an adaptation process, but generic composability/current-task research does not donate S4 ownership to the frozen standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish and preserve the durable workspace contract that defines project-level standing instructions and advance authorization governing later LiteCode coding behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: inconsistent project rules, missing durable authorization, divergent coding conventions and later agent behavior that no longer reflects the legitimate project owner&#39;s standing constraints. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what persistent project instructions, boundaries and authorization should govern LiteCode agents working in this workspace. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Parent (P) — the legitimate project owner/operator owns the authoritative CLAUDE.md workspace contract. No separate first-party autonomous S5 policy-authoring mode is established. - Return-to-operation path: LiteCode initializes/recognizes root CLAUDE.md; each new turn re-reads it from disk through contractsnapshotforturn; buildsystemprompt splices the contract into every non-hidden agent&#39;s system prompt; later model/tool behavior is therefore governed by the returned owner-selected policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the semantic content of the durable workspace contract, including project instructions and advance authorization boundaries. - Decision owner: the legitimate project owner/operator at the parent recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workspace initialization shell; workspace editor/file persistence; per-turn contract snapshot; system-prompt splicing; permission engine and built-in prompts that explicitly honor durable CLAUDE.md authorization. - Closure path: project policy/authorization issue → owner writes/edits root CLAUDE.md → file persists → the next turn re-reads the fresh contract → LiteCode injects it into the model&#39;s system context → subsequent operation follows that returned standing policy. - Boundary reachability: workspace initialization creates the contract surface in ordinary projects; the standard runtime re-reads it for every turn and the desktop/browser product includes direct workspace editing. No external governance adapter is required for the parent decision to return into operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary agents can technically edit files, but the shipped prompt treats CLAUDE.md as durable user/project authorization rather than an agent-owned self-authorization channel. The decisive ultimate-policy right therefore remains with the legitimate project owner. - Evidence: src/config/workspace.rs; src/contextpipeline/env.rs; src/contextpipeline/system.rs; src/runtime/mod.rs; src/config/globaldb/builtinprompts.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is project-recursion S5, not ownership of LiteCode&#39;s product-level safety model or provider configuration. A user asking an agent to edit the contract does not by itself transfer ultimate authority away from the user.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c496ccc28c34c2726778e33a905ccc589cc832c3/assessments/litecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c496ccc28c34c2726778e33a905ccc589cc832c3\">c496ccc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T13:30:04Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/crewlet/crewlet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Crewlet</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/crewlet/crewlet/tree/346d58ee3f3627a7f1213e441c6b0e330ab48030\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 346d58ee3f3627a7f1213e441c6b0e330ab48030\">346d58e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform real organizational work through autonomous per-seat agent turns. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user/work-item requests, external tool state, provider responses, tool errors, knowledge gaps, interruptions, budget limits and results encountered while completing a seat&#39;s assigned work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool/action or response after observing current task context and prior tool results. - Decision owner: the model-driven agent seat in the Executor phase. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-seat runner, tool loop, MCP/builtin surfaces, collaboration/skill guards, budgets, delegation guards, inbox/event routing, sandbox suspension/resume and delivery verification. - Closure path: trigger → Executor model decision → first-party tool execution → result appended to the same turn → later model decision → submitted work → reviewer/engine disposition and external result. - Boundary reachability: this is the normal shipped seat execution path; optional external coding agents are not required for the positive S1 path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic engine code constrains and records execution, but the seat model owns substantive next-action choice in the work loop. - Evidence: docs/concepts/agent-runtime.md; docs/concepts/turn-engine.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider-side model internals remain external; credit rests on the first-party closed agent/tool execution path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate conflicting concurrent mutation of shared current work among distinct agent-seat S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two seats can race to change the same native work item, producing incompatible concurrent state if writes are accepted independently. - Distinct S1 units: independently executing Crewlet agent seats operating against the same company&#39;s native tracker. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent writers targeting one work item compete over the same shared work state. - Attenuating coordination relation: the native tracker publishes writes onto the item&#39;s ordered shared log; the broker arbitrates competing writes so two writers racing on one task contend at one ordering point and exactly one wins, while unrelated tasks do not contend. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: write results expose applied, pending, or unknown, nodes replay the same ordered record, and subsequent reads/notifications present the serialized work state back to seats for later action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is tied to an explicit same-item concurrent-write conflict and a mechanism specifically preventing divergent acceptance, not to Crewlet&#39;s generic bus, hierarchy, task routing, or delegation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: deterministic shared-log/broker arbitration establishes which competing mutation wins and therefore which state later S1s observe. - Decision owner: no autonomous agent owns the arbitration decision for a concrete write race; the first-party runtime supplies the function-specific deterministic coordination path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordered native work log, per-object contention, applied-position tracking, operation identity and same-write read-after-apply behavior. - Closure path: concurrent S1 writes → shared ordered arbitration → one canonical ordered state/outcome → replicated tracker state and notifications → later S1 decisions use that state. - Boundary reachability: this path is part of the shipped native tracker backend, one of Crewlet&#39;s documented standard work modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: S2 itself is materially closed first-party, but the collision resolution is deterministic rather than selected by an autonomous coordination actor, so constructor credit is appropriate. - Evidence: docs/concepts/task-engine.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: lead assignment, mailboxes and the generic coordination store are not independently credited as S2; the positive witness is the explicit concurrent shared-work collision path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current team commitments and allocation across active operational seats, with both autonomous lead-agent and human-lead modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new work, blocked work, changing report capacity/fit, parallel task progress and completed/failed results require present-tense assignment, reassignment, review or intervention for the team as a whole. - Whole-system current view: at team recursion the lead receives a roster of direct reports and can inspect the team&#39;s current tracker/work surfaces; assignment and transition notifications return current work changes to the relevant lead/manager. - Current-control decision scope: choose which report receives work, create/assign subtasks, react to blockers and progress, review results, revise current commitments and close/transition parent work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in agent-led mode the lead model reasons over roster/current work and chooses allocation/intervention; in human-led mode the human lead makes the same current-control decisions through the PM/chat surfaces and those changes wake affected agent seats. - Decision owner: autonomous lead agent in the base mode; legitimate human lead in the first-party parent-governed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: executable org hierarchy, manages/unit lead relationships, lead roster prompt, native/external tracker routing, assignment webhooks, colleague tools, human-seat identity/routing and event delivery. - Closure path: current work/report state → lead decision → tracker/chat assignment or intervention → affected seat wakes/changes current work → progress/transition returns to lead and informs later control. - Boundary reachability: both agent leads and human leads are explicitly supported standard organization modes; human seats can lead units and agent work returns through the same integrated work surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base task-allocation choice is explicitly described as lead-agent reasoning rather than routing code; the parent mode separately gives that decision right to a human lead and returns the result through first-party routing. - Evidence: docs/concepts/organization-model.md; docs/concepts/task-engine.md; docs/concepts/humans-in-the-org.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a lead title or one-shot delegation is not the witness; credit rests on the documented current-work allocation/review loop and its return through work-state notifications.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an operational seat&#39;s claim about completed work using a separate reviewer with direct engine-recorded evidence and return the judgment into the same turn&#39;s control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an Executor can claim delivery or quality that its actual tool record does not support, or can produce plausible prose while failing to reach the required external surface. - Claim being audited: whether the round&#39;s work is actually good enough to finish and consistent with what the engine recorded as executed/delivered. - Ordinary reporting path: Executor&#39;s own submitwork outcome, summary, claimed deliveries and produced text. - Complementary access path: a separate Reviewer prompt/model receives the round&#39;s verbatim engine-recorded tool log and work text on a tool surface with no domain tools; engine-side delivery checks additionally compare claims to recorded calls and destinations. - Independence boundary: Reviewer has its own prompt/model phase and narrower tool surface, and the evidence includes engine-recorded calls rather than only the Executor&#39;s self-report. It is therefore materially distinct from ordinary production reporting even though it is routinely invoked. - Who acts on findings: Reviewer can return selfiterate or failed; selfiterate sends the turn back to a fresh Executor round with the prior-work ledger, and engine overrides can also force correction when a claimed delivery did not reach the waiting party. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the reviewer model owns the quality judgment among done, selfiterate, and failed, subject to stricter deterministic delivery truth checks. - Decision owner: autonomous Reviewer model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: phase-separated prompts/models, forced submitreview, engine-recorded call ledger, delivery Check/OverrideDone, prior-work ledger, stall/iteration guards. - Closure path: Executor result + direct execution record → separate Reviewer judgment → selfiterate/failed/done → next Executor round or termination → later operation reflects the audit finding. - Boundary reachability: Execute→Review is the documented ordinary turn engine, not a development-only evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic checks enforce facts such as whether a write landed, while the separate reviewer model owns the substantive quality/challenge judgment that can send work back. - Evidence: README.md; docs/concepts/turn-engine.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the positive mapping does not rely on a component merely named reviewer; it relies on separate access to engine-recorded reality plus corrective return into execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: derive durable future-operating adaptations from completed work, with an autonomous per-seat learning mode and a parent-governed team-knowledge promotion mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated external work patterns, failures and successful tool trajectories reveal procedures or knowledge that should change how later turns operate; cross-seat convergence can reveal team-level practice worth adopting. - External distinction: learning consumes settled operational turns containing actual tool sequences, review outcomes, counterpart interactions and task results against the company&#39;s work environment rather than only internal planning text. - Future / prospective distinction: synthesized skills, diary/episodes and promoted team practices are retained specifically for future turns; clustered synthesis searches repeated historical trajectories for reusable patterns not needed merely to finish the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: auxiliary-model synthesis drafts reusable per-seat skills; cross-agent promotion distils convergence among distinct seats into a team knowledge draft. - Path back into current capability / S3: synthesized skills are stored per seat and exposed to later turns through the skill catalogue/useskill; a parent-published cross-agent draft becomes an ordinary team knowledge page reachable by future query-time search. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in base mode an auxiliary model can draft/decline a reusable skill and first-party learning stores the admitted result; in parent mode a person/lead decides whether an auto-drafted cross-agent practice is published into live team knowledge. - Decision owner: autonomous auxiliary learning model in the base mode; human/parent reviewer for cross-agent team-practice publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: post-turn Reflector, episodes, PersistDecider, Synthesizer, duplicate/cap gates, clustered synthesis, Promoter, hidden auto-draft subtree, synthesized-skill store, query/prefetch and useskill/refineskill tools. - Closure path: completed operational evidence → synthesis/proposal → durable skill or parent-reviewed team draft → future prompt/search/tool access → later agent behavior changes. - Boundary reachability: post-turn learning is a shipped engine subsystem; cross-agent promotion is a shipped background duty and publication path, not repository-development learning. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-based synthesis supplies autonomous future-oriented adaptation in the base mode; the separate team-promotion path deliberately holds publication for parent/human judgment rather than silently sharing agent-generated policy. - Evidence: docs/concepts/agent-learning.md; docs/concepts/agent-runtime.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: generic memory or reflection is not the witness; credit rests on evidence-derived reusable skill/practice options with explicit reuse in later operation. The team promotion path remains parent-governed because the draft is hidden until a person publishes it.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: close company identity and ultimate-policy decisions through founder/operator authority and propagate those decisions back into subsequent agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: questions that change what the company is for or what ultimate policies govern it — mission, vision, company-wide policies and top-level direction — exceed ordinary seat-level task authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Tier-B configuration explicitly defines company name, mission, vision and policies; top agents can escalate decisions above their authority to a root human founder whose documented responsibility is to own direction/final calls. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent-governed only — the legitimate founder/operator owns Tier-B company configuration through authenticated API/operator credentials; the founder human seat supplies the organizational escalation identity while configuration authority remains the distinct operator hat. - Return-to-operation path: founder/operator decision → versioned PUT/PATCH /config or import of the Tier-B document → activation pointer/control-plane apply across nodes → new epoch → later Executor prompts render the updated mission, vision and full policy text and agents operate under it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide and authorize company identity/ultimate-policy changes and activate the resulting company revision. - Decision owner: legitimate parent human founder/operator; no autonomous Crewlet agent is shown holding ultimate configuration authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: human founder seat at org root, escalation via normal colleague surfaces, authenticated API tokens, versioned Tier-B config, validation, activation pointer, fleet reconcile/hot reload and per-turn epoch pinning. - Closure path: identity/policy matter reaches founder → founder decides and exercises operator configuration authority → live revision propagates → subsequent agent turns receive changed mission/vision/policies. - Boundary reachability: root human founder seats and live founder-owned Tier-B configuration are both documented first-party deployment surfaces; the docs explicitly distinguish the founder&#39;s colleague seat from the operator credential rather than conflating them. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ultimate identity authority is intentionally human/parent-owned. Static mission/policy text alone would not earn S5; credit comes from the live authenticated parent decision-and-return path. - Evidence: docs/concepts/humans-in-the-org.md; docs/concepts/configuration.md; docs/concepts/agent-runtime.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary task approval is not S5. The positive claim is restricted to founder-owned live identity/ultimate-policy fields and their explicit propagation into later runtime behavior.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/38590686cb3f3c95b389e5c051cad9a1cf1b6a4c/assessments/crewlet.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 38590686cb3f3c95b389e5c051cad9a1cf1b6a4c\">3859068 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
